window.LX_DIAPOS = [{"id":"seance-1","numero":1,"titre":"First class: presenting the course and defining AI","resume":"Define AI through observable tasks, say whether an everyday object involves AI with a justification, and describe what the imitation game measures.","duree":"1 h 30 min","lien":"cours.html#chapitre-1","fiche":"enseignant.html#guide-seance-1","diapos":[{"type":"titre","surtitre":"Artificial Intelligence · Session 1","titre":"First class: presenting the course and defining AI","sousTitre":"Master in General and Comparative Literature · Semester 3 · Université Yahia Farès, Médéa · 2025–2026","notes":"While you set up, write an opening question on the board: “Can a machine read a novel?” Do not settle it: it will come back with the imitation game. If the room allows, show the site’s home page, which computes the sentiment of a sentence: what does “reading” mean for a machine? Equipment to check: projector, the site open at the course card, printed questionnaire, strips of paper."},{"type":"points","titre":"Session objectives","points":["**Define** AI through observable tasks","**Say** whether an everyday object involves AI, with a justification","**Describe** the imitation game and what it measures"],"notes":"Read the three objectives aloud: each one starts with an action verb, so you can check at the end that it has been reached. Announce the outline: presenting the course, a questionnaire, an “AI or not AI?” vote, teacher input, then a game in which two students play the human and the machine."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"The course in one semester","entetes":["Part","Content","Duration"],"lignes":[["Chapter 1","Introduction: defining AI, history, AI agents, types of learning","1 week"],["Chapter 2","Prompt engineering & Python: fundamentals, text files, pandas","2 weeks"],["Chapter 3","Machine learning, deep learning (NLP), AI assistants for text analysis","6 weeks"],["Mini-project","Guided independent work: applying agents and Python to literature/sociology","3 weeks"],["Tutorials (TD) / labs (TP)","Prompt crafting, exercises with solutions, then Python programming","All semester"],["Round tables","4 debates: origins, methods in SHS, new issues, outlook","About 45 min"]],"legende":"Durations set by the official syllabus; the exact calendar is set by the teacher.","notes":"15 min for this slide and the next. Show the course card and the calendar on the site. In front of the class, settle the two points that the syllabus leaves open: how the assessment is split, according to your faculty’s regulations in force, and the number of mini-project subjects (“1 subject of your choice” or “at least 3 of the 5 proposed subjects”). Announce the rule you have chosen today."},{"type":"points","titre":"How to work in this course","points":["Programming starts from **zero**: no prior knowledge of computing is needed","About **3 hours** of personal work a week, done regularly","**Test yourself** instead of rereading; **space out** your revision","Work on each tutorial exercise **before** opening the solution","Learn each term in **French** and in **English**"],"notes":"Open the “How to learn this course” page and show the “A typical week” table: five moments, three hours in all. Stress this point: a review of research on the psychology of learning (Dunlosky et al., 2013) ranks testing yourself and spacing out revision among the most useful techniques; rereading and highlighting give a feeling of mastery, but little of it stays. Reassure students about Python: we start from zero."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Diagnostic questionnaire","duree":10,"consignes":["Have you ever used a chatbot?","Have you ever written code?","Do you have a laptop? An Internet connection at home?","In which language do you prefer to read technical documentation?","What do you expect from this course?"],"materiel":"The printed questionnaire, one sheet per student","notes":"10 min. Collect the sheets without commenting on the answers in public. The answers about equipment will help you form the pairs for the lab sessions (TP): put each student who has no computer with a student who has one. The preferred reading language will tell you which students to point toward the English or Arabic version of the site."},{"type":"activite","titre":"AI or not AI? Vote","duree":15,"consignes":["For each object, vote by a show of hands: AI or not AI?","Calculator · spellchecker · spam filter · machine translator","Thermostat · search engine · GPS · chatbot","Spot the objects that divide the class","A volunteer from each side justifies their vote in one sentence"],"notes":"15 min. Count the votes and write the results on the board; keep them for the end of the teacher input. Give no answers: the aim is to bring disagreements to light. Conclusion to draw: if we disagree, we first need to define what we call “artificial intelligence”."},{"type":"section","numero":1,"titre":"What is artificial intelligence?","sousTitre":"Dartmouth, agents, a working definition","notes":"20 min of teacher input, based on the lesson “What is artificial intelligence?” in chapter 1. Announce that you will then come back to the eight objects the class voted on."},{"type":"chiffre","valeur":"1956","legende":"The Dartmouth workshop: the term “artificial intelligence” becomes the name of a research field","source":"Proposal written in 1955 by McCarthy, Minsky, Rochester and Shannon","notes":"Tell the story: in 1955, John McCarthy, Marvin Minsky, Nathaniel Rochester and Claude Shannon asked for funding to bring researchers together at Dartmouth College, in the United States, in the summer of 1956. Their hypothesis: every aspect of learning and intelligence could be described precisely enough for a machine to simulate it. This workshop is considered the birth of the field. Add straight away that there is no single definition: there is no agreement on what the word “intelligence” itself means."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Agent","traduction":"agent","definition":"A system that perceives its environment and acts on it to achieve a goal.","exemple":"A spam filter “perceives” the text of an email and “acts” by putting it in the right folder.","notes":"This is how the standard textbook by Stuart Russell and Peter Norvig, *Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach* (1995), presents AI: as the study of agents. Ask for another agent among the eight objects voted on. Possible answers: the GPS perceives a position and suggests a route; the thermostat perceives the temperature and acts on the heating. Point out that this definition is very broad: it is not enough to settle the question."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Artificial intelligence","traduction":"intelligence artificielle (IA)","definition":"The set of computing methods that allow a machine to perform tasks we usually associate with human intelligence: recognizing, classifying, predicting, reasoning, planning, understanding or producing language.","exemple":"Recognizing the characters of an old newspaper (OCR), translating between Arabic and French, sorting emails.","notes":"This is the course’s working definition. Stress that it is about observable tasks: it says nothing about what the machine “understands”. Add that talking about “AI” in the singular is misleading: it is a family of techniques (rules written by experts, exploring possibilities, learning from examples, neural networks). Have students write down the question to ask about any result produced by “an AI”: which technique, trained on which data, for which task?"},{"type":"deux","titre":"Why AI matters for literary studies","colonnes":[{"titre":"Roberto Busa, 1949","points":["An Italian Jesuit indexes the works of Thomas Aquinas, with help from IBM","Often given as the origin of the **digital humanities** (*humanités numériques*)"]},{"titre":"Franco Moretti, 2000 and 2013","points":["No one can read every novel published in the 19th century","**Distant reading** (*lecture distante*): thousands of texts, measurable features","It does not replace close reading"]}],"notes":"For a comparatist, Moretti is the reference: “Conjectures on World Literature” (2000), then *Distant Reading* (2013). Measurable features: genres, titles, character networks, the movement of forms from one language to another. Two concrete uses from chapter 1: in 2019, Florian Cafiero and Jean-Baptiste Camps provided quantitative arguments in favor of Molière in the controversy that set him against Corneille; you can also align a novel by Naguib Mahfouz sentence by sentence with its French and English translations, to compare the translators’ choices."},{"type":"deux","titre":"Back to the eight objects","colonnes":[{"titre":"Examples the course uses","points":["Spam filter: an agent that sorts emails","Machine translator: a narrow AI","Search engine: AI in everyday life","GPS: searching for a route among possibilities","Chatbot: a large language model"]},{"titre":"To discuss: which technique?","points":["Calculator: is calculating an “intelligent” task?","Thermostat: it perceives and acts; is that enough?","Spellchecker: a dictionary, or a learned model?"]}],"notes":"Go back to the votes on the board. For each object, ask the question of the definition: does it perform a task associated with human intelligence (recognizing, classifying, predicting, planning, producing language)? The first five are examples from chapter 1, where planning a route between Médéa and Algiers illustrates “state-space search”. For the other three, accept both answers if they are justified by the technique assumed: what counts is the justification, not the verdict."},{"type":"section","numero":2,"titre":"The imitation game","sousTitre":"Alan Turing, 1950","notes":"25 min in all: the short version of the activity in the guide, with two witnesses, five minutes of questions, a vote and a discussion."},{"type":"citation","texte":"I propose to consider the question, “Can machines think?”","source":"Alan M. Turing, “Computing Machinery and Intelligence”, *Mind*, 1950","notes":"This is the first sentence of the article, quoted here in Turing’s own words (the French slides give the course’s translation). Explain Turing’s move: rather than defining “thinking”, he proposes an observable test. In his original game, an interrogator must guess in writing who is the man and who is the woman; then a machine takes one of the places. If the interrogator cannot tell the machine from the human, the machine is behaving intelligently: this is the “Turing test”."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Let’s play: which one is the machine?","duree":25,"consignes":["Two witnesses, out of sight: X answers sincerely, Y plays the machine","The class asks its questions in writing, on strips of paper","Five minutes of questions per witness; the teacher reads the answers aloud","Show of hands: which one is the machine? Then the reveal","Discussion: what gave the machine away?"],"materiel":"Strips of paper, a place out of sight, the rule card given to Y","notes":"Timing: roles 3 min, questions 10 min (five minutes per witness, the length of time Turing mentions), vote 5 min, discussion for the rest. Discreetly give Y the rule card from the next slide, which you will only project after the vote. Read all the answers yourself: handwriting would give the witnesses away. Variation: Y is a chatbot whose answers you read out; the question then becomes “what does deceiving prove?”."},{"type":"points","titre":"The reveal: the “machine’s” rule card","points":["The question contains “you” → “Why are you asking me that question?”","It contains a word about family → “Tell me about your family.”","It asks for a calculation → wait thirty seconds, then answer","Otherwise → “I am not sure I understand. Could you rephrase that?”"],"notes":"Project it only after the vote. These rules are in the spirit of ELIZA, the program by Joseph Weizenbaum (MIT, 1966) that imitated a psychotherapist by rephrasing the user’s sentences. Ask: what gave the machine away? Should it imitate our mistakes? In Turing’s example, the machine answers an addition after about thirty seconds, and gets it wrong."},{"type":"question","titre":"Discussion","question":"Is deceiving the interrogator the same as thinking?","reponse":"Not necessarily: Turing replaces a philosophical question with an observable test. Passing the test does not prove understanding.","explication":"This is the objection raised by the philosopher John Searle (1980) with the Chinese room, which we will see in session 2.","notes":"10 min of discussion. Let both sides speak before showing the answer. Point to bring out: the test measures a behavior, the ability to pass for a human in writing; it does not define intelligence and does not prove understanding."},{"type":"definition","terme":"ELIZA effect","traduction":"effet ELIZA","definition":"The tendency to attribute intelligence, or even real understanding, to a program that talks.","exemple":"In 1966, users of ELIZA, which merely rephrased their sentences with simple rules, believed it truly understood them.","notes":"Link it to the game: if the class hesitated over Y, who applied only four rules, that is the ELIZA effect. It also applies to today’s chatbots: answering well does not prove understanding."},{"type":"points","titre":"Common mistakes to correct","points":["Reducing AI to robots or to recent chatbots","Believing that a system understands because it answers well: this is the **ELIZA effect**","Taking the Turing test for a **definition** of intelligence"],"notes":"Reread these three mistakes and ask, for each one, what in the session helps to correct it. Expected: the definition through tasks and the family of techniques (1); the “machine’s” rule card (2); the fact that Turing proposes an observable test, not a definition (3)."},{"type":"fin","titre":"Wrap-up and homework","points":[{"texte":"**Exit ticket** (5 min)","sous":["One thing you learned today","One question you still have"]},{"texte":"**To read** on the site","sous":["“What is artificial intelligence?” (chapter 1)","“A typical week” (How to learn this course)"]},"**To note**: one use of AI you met this week, with the technique it probably relies on"],"texte":"Session 2: history of AI, weak AI and strong AI","notes":"Collect the tickets and read them the same evening: the best questions will open session 2. Before ending the class, check the objectives with two quick questions: “Define AI in one sentence.” “What does the imitation game measure?”"}]},{"id":"seance-2","numero":2,"titre":"History of AI, weak AI and strong AI","resume":"Put about ten milestones in the history of AI in order and place the two winters, distinguish narrow from general AI and weak from strong AI, and summarize the Chinese room in three sentences.","duree":"1 h 30 min","lien":"cours.html#c1-histoire","fiche":"enseignant.html#guide-seance-2","diapos":[{"type":"titre","surtitre":"Artificial Intelligence · Session 2","titre":"History of AI, weak AI and strong AI","sousTitre":"Chapter 1 · Historical overview, narrow and general AI, the Chinese room","notes":"Before the session: prepare one card per milestone of the chapter 1 timeline (the event on the front, the date on the back), magnets and markers in two colors. Draw an empty timeline on the board, from 1940 to today. Reread the exit tickets from session 1 to choose the three review questions."},{"type":"points","titre":"Session objectives","points":["**Put** about ten milestones in the history of AI in order and place the two winters","**Distinguish** narrow AI from general AI, and weak AI from strong AI","**Summarize** the Chinese room argument in three sentences"],"notes":"Announce the outline: a review, a timeline built by the class, two pairs of notions not to be confused, then a short debate on machine translation."},{"type":"points","titre":"Review: three questions, from memory","progressif":true,"points":["In what year, and where, did the term “artificial intelligence” become established?","Who proposed the “imitation game”, and in what year?","What is the “ELIZA effect”?"],"notes":"5 min. Replace these questions with three questions taken from the session 1 exit tickets if you have better ones. Students answer from memory, without notes. Expected answers: in 1956, at the Dartmouth workshop; Alan Turing, in 1950; the tendency to attribute real understanding to a program that talks, as ELIZA’s users did (1966)."},{"type":"section","numero":1,"titre":"A class timeline","sousTitre":"From 1943 to today: date, place, color","notes":"30 min, correction included. The correction relies on the two timeline slides that follow the activity."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Dating and placing the cards","duree":30,"consignes":["Each group receives a few cards: the event on the front, the date on the back","Date your cards without looking at the back","Place them on the timeline on the board","Correction: turn the cards over and move the ones that are misplaced","Color them: one color for symbolic AI, another for connectionism","Mark the two “AI winters”"],"materiel":"One card per milestone of the chapter 1 timeline, magnets, markers in two colors","notes":"Share out the cards so that each group has three or four. While the cards are being placed, do not correct: ask each group to justify one of its dates. Before coloring, recall the two families of ideas: the symbolic approach programs explicit rules and reasoning; the connectionist approach trains artificial neural networks."},{"type":"chronologie","titre":"Correction: from the pioneers to the first winter","evenements":[{"date":"1943","texte":"McCulloch and Pitts: the formal neuron, ancestor of neural networks"},{"date":"1950","texte":"Turing proposes the imitation game, the future “Turing test”"},{"date":"1956","texte":"The Dartmouth workshop; Newell and Simon’s Logic Theorist"},{"date":"1958","texte":"Rosenblatt’s perceptron; its limits are shown in 1969"},{"date":"1966","texte":"ELIZA (Weizenbaum, MIT) imitates a psychotherapist"},{"date":"1973","texte":"The Lighthill report: first AI winter, until around 1980"},{"date":"1970s–1980s","texte":"The age of expert systems, such as MYCIN at Stanford"}],"notes":"Correct card by card, asking for the color of each one. Connectionism: 1943 and 1958. Symbolic: the Logic Theorist, which proves theorems in logic; ELIZA, which rephrases with simple rules; expert systems, which reason with a few hundred “if… then…” rules. The imitation game is a test, not a technique: no color. The limits of the perceptron were shown by Minsky and Papert in *Perceptrons* (1969). First winter: funding fell, first in the United Kingdom and then in the United States."},{"type":"chronologie","titre":"Correction: from the second winter to large models","evenements":[{"date":"1987","texte":"Second winter: the market for Lisp machines collapses"},{"date":"1997","texte":"Deep Blue (IBM) beats Garry Kasparov, the world chess champion"},{"date":"2012","texte":"The AlexNet network clearly outperforms other methods in the ImageNet competition"},{"date":"2016","texte":"AlphaGo (DeepMind) beats Lee Sedol at Go, by four games to one"},{"date":"2017","texte":"“Attention Is All You Need”: the Transformer architecture"},{"date":"2022","texte":"November 30: OpenAI opens ChatGPT to the public"},{"date":"2024","texte":"The European AI regulation (AI Act): systems classified by level of risk"}],"notes":"Second winter: expensive to maintain and fragile outside their domain, expert systems disappointed; budgets shrank until the early 1990s. Colors: AlexNet and the Transformer are connectionist; AlphaGo combines neural networks, reinforcement learning and the exploration of a tree of moves. Trap: Deep Blue did not learn; it explored millions of positions very quickly. The 2018 card, if you made one: BERT (Google), a pre-trained model, and OpenAI’s first GPT."},{"type":"deux","titre":"Two families of ideas, two colors","colonnes":[{"titre":"The symbolic approach","points":["Programming explicit rules and reasoning","1956: the Logic Theorist · 1966: ELIZA","1970s–1980s: expert systems"]},{"titre":"The connectionist approach","points":["Training artificial neural networks","1943: the formal neuron · 1958: the perceptron","2012: AlexNet · 2017: the Transformer"]}],"notes":"Have students copy this table: it sums up the timeline. Ask where to place AlphaGo (2016). Expected: on both sides, since it combines neural networks and search in a tree of moves; we speak of a hybrid approach. Deep Blue, for its part, belongs to state-space search."},{"type":"chiffre","valeur":"1958 → 1997","legende":"Simon and Newell predict a computer world chess champion within ten years. It will take nearly forty.","notes":"10 min on what history teaches. First ask: how long did it actually take? From the prediction by Herbert Simon and Allen Newell (1958) to Deep Blue’s victory (1997), nearly forty years. Each technical wave brings predictions that are too optimistic."},{"type":"points","titre":"Cycles of hope and disappointment","points":[{"texte":"Two **AI winters**","sous":["1973: the Lighthill report, until around 1980","1987: the collapse of Lisp machines, until the early 1990s"]},{"texte":"Abandoned ideas come back","sous":["Neural networks, judged to have no future in 1969","Back in 1986 with backpropagation, dominant after 2012"]},"**A lesson in method**: judge a system on what it does, measured by tests, not on what people promise"],"notes":"Backpropagation of the gradient comes from David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton and Ronald Williams (1986). Have students apply the lesson in method to a current chatbot: how should it be judged? Expected: on tests, for example by checking in a catalog the references it suggests."},{"type":"section","numero":2,"titre":"Two pairs of notions","sousTitre":"Narrow or general AI · weak or strong AI","notes":"20 min: the two distinctions, the comparison table, then the Chinese room, which you tell and the students then summarize."},{"type":"deux","titre":"Two distinctions not to confuse","colonnes":[{"titre":"Narrow or general: the scope","points":["One specific task, or all human intellectual tasks?","A **technical** distinction"]},{"titre":"Weak or strong: the mind","points":["Simulating intelligence, or really possessing a mind?","A **philosophical** distinction (John Searle, 1980)"]}],"notes":"The syllabus pairs the two: “narrow (or weak) AI”, “general (or strong) AI”, and everyday usage mixes them up. Stress this: the first question is “what can the system do?”, the second “does it have a mind?”. The course example: the program that beats chess champions cannot read a poem; it is a narrow AI."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Narrow AI and general AI: a comparison","entetes":["Criterion","Narrow (weak) AI","General (strong) AI"],"lignes":[["Definition","Designed and trained for one specific task","Hypothetical, as versatile as a human"],["Examples","Deep Blue, AlphaGo, machine translation","No recognized real example; HAL 9000 in science fiction"],["State in 2026","Everywhere: search engines, phones, SHS","A stated goal; no consensus that it has been achieved"],["Question","Is succeeding at a task the same as understanding it?","Can a machine think, have intentions?"]],"legende":"Based on the comparison table in chapter 1","notes":"Have students read the table row by row, then hide it and ask them to rebuild it from memory: this is exactly what the exam and the round tables ask for. What we would expect from a general AI: transferring its knowledge from one domain to another, learning a new task from very few examples and, in Searle’s sense, truly understanding what it processes. HAL 9000 is the computer in *2001: A Space Odyssey*."},{"type":"etapes","titre":"The Chinese room (Searle, 1980)","progressif":true,"etapes":[{"titre":"A closed room","texte":"A person who does not know Chinese, with a manual written in their own language"},{"titre":"Messages in Chinese","texte":"Chinese characters are slipped to them through a slot"},{"titre":"The manual","texte":"It tells them which characters to send back in reply"},{"titre":"Seen from outside","texte":"The room seems to answer perfectly in Chinese"},{"titre":"And yet","texte":"The person understands nothing: syntax, without semantics"}],"notes":"Tell the thought experiment step by step; it comes from the article “Minds, Brains, and Programs” (1980). Searle’s conclusion: in the same way, a program manipulates symbols according to their form (syntax) without reaching their meaning (semantics); so passing the Turing test would not prove understanding. Also give his critics’ reply: it is the system as a whole that would understand. The debate remains open."},{"type":"question","titre":"Your turn","question":"Summarize the Chinese room argument in three sentences.","reponse":"A person who does not know Chinese answers messages in Chinese using a rule manual. Seen from outside, the answers are perfect, yet the person understands nothing. In the same way, a program manipulates symbols without grasping their meaning: passing the Turing test does not prove understanding.","explication":"Three steps: the situation, the observation, the conclusion about programs.","notes":"5 min in silence, then have two or three summaries read out before showing the answer. Check that the third sentence generalizes to programs: that is what turns the story into an argument."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Debate: does a machine translator understand?","duree":20,"consignes":["The question: “Does a machine translator understand what it translates?”","Two assigned sides: yes or no","Two speakers per side","Then questions from the floor"],"notes":"Assign the sides yourself, regardless of opinions: defending a position you do not share forces you to listen to the other side. Ideas from the course to slip in if the debate runs out of steam: the Chinese room; the translation tools that for a long time rendered a gender-neutral Turkish sentence as “he is a doctor” but “she is a nurse”; Emily Bender and her co-authors (2021), for whom large language models are “stochastic parrots” that recombine forms they have learned."},{"type":"deux","titre":"Concluding: two separate questions","colonnes":[{"titre":"The observable","points":["Is the translation correct?","It can be checked: we compare, we test","A question of **narrow** AI: a well-defined task"]},{"titre":"The philosophical","points":["Is there understanding?","The result alone cannot tell us: the Chinese room","A question of **weak or strong** AI: the debate remains open"]}],"notes":"Conclude without naming a winner, by separating the two questions. Widen the discussion to large language models: they write, summarize and translate on almost any subject, but they are trained first to predict how a sequence of words continues, and they make mistakes that a human would not make. There is no consensus that they are general AI: they are powerful but specialized tools, and everything they produce must be checked."},{"type":"points","titre":"Common mistakes to correct","points":["“Weak AI” does not mean “performing poorly”: Deep Blue beat the world champion","Deep Blue did not learn: it explored millions of positions","Large language models are not general AI"],"notes":"For each mistake, ask a student to correct it with an element from the timeline or the table. “Weak” is the opposite of “strong”, that is, it concerns the question of mind, not “performing well”. On language models: there is no scientific consensus that general AI has been achieved."},{"type":"fin","titre":"Wrap-up and homework","points":["**Wrap-up** (5 min): write down three dates you will remember, and why",{"texte":"**Homework**","sous":["Build a personal timeline of ten dates","Group concerned: prepare the round table “The origins and emergence of AI”"]}],"texte":"Session 3: tutorial (TD), supervised or unsupervised?","notes":"Collect the three dates: if a key date is often missing, come back to it at the start of the next session. The timeline method is described in “How to approach each part”: ten dates are enough. Remember the prerequisite for session 3: have students read the chapter 1 lesson “Supervised learning and unsupervised learning”."}]},{"id":"seance-3","numero":3,"titre":"Chapter 1 tutorial (TD): supervised or unsupervised?","resume":"Classify at least 8 situations out of 10 (supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement) using the labels as justification, explain the role of the test set and give two examples in SHS.","duree":"1 h 30 min","lien":"cours.html#td-ch1","fiche":"enseignant.html#guide-seance-3","diapos":[{"type":"titre","surtitre":"Artificial Intelligence · Session 3 · Tutorial (TD)","titre":"Chapter 1 tutorial (TD): supervised or unsupervised?","sousTitre":"Sorting situations, comparing, finding examples","notes":"Prerequisite: the chapter 1 lesson “Supervised learning and unsupervised learning”. Prepare ten situation cards per group (the table in the lesson plan) and open the exercises of the chapter 1 tutorial (TD) on the site. Form groups of three or four, mixing levels and equipment."},{"type":"points","titre":"Session objectives","points":["**Classify** at least 8 situations out of 10: supervised, unsupervised or reinforcement","**Justify** each choice using the labels","**Explain** the role of the test set","**Give** two examples of learning in the humanities and social sciences"],"notes":"The first objective has a numerical criterion: 8 situations out of 10. Announce it: it will give the groups a benchmark during the sorting."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Sorting ten situation cards","duree":25,"consignes":["In groups of three or four","Sort the ten cards into three piles: supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement","On each card, write down the decisive clue","Each group then defends one card in front of the class"],"materiel":"Ten situation cards per group","notes":"During the sorting, walk around and ask “what have you tried?” rather than giving the answer. Two cards are traps (Go and the spam filter): do not point them out. For the defense, draw the spokesperson at random: everyone must be ready. Only show the correction after the defenses."},{"type":"deux","titre":"Correction: eight cards without a trap","colonnes":[{"titre":"Supervised: the examples carry the answer","points":["Dating a poem using 600 dated poems","Estimating the year a poem was written (regression)","Classifying 3,000 speeches using 500 classified speeches","Detecting hateful comments using 5,000 annotated examples","Identifying the language of a message using labeled messages"]},{"titre":"Unsupervised: groups to interpret","points":["Dividing 200 novels into stylistic families","Grouping 2,000 open-ended answers without a grid","Bringing out the themes of 1,000 articles"]}],"notes":"For each card, have students state the clue: dated, classified, annotated or labeled examples → supervised; no grid, groups or themes to discover → unsupervised. The year of composition is a numerical label: this is regression, not classification. For the 3,000 speeches, recall the quiz in the lesson: it is normal that the new speeches have no label, because that is exactly what the model must predict."},{"type":"question","titre":"Trap no. 1","question":"“Learning Go by winning or losing games”: what type of learning is this?","choix":["Supervised","Unsupervised","Reinforcement"],"reponse":"Reinforcement","explication":"There are no corrected examples: the program plays, and winning or losing serves as its reward or penalty.","notes":"Likely wrong answer: “unsupervised”, because there are no labels. Show the difference: here, the program learns by acting and receiving rewards. This is how AlphaGo Zero (DeepMind, 2017) learned Go by playing against itself. Reinforcement learning will be studied in chapter 3."},{"type":"question","titre":"Trap no. 2","question":"A filter learns from the emails that users have marked as “spam”. What type of learning is this?","choix":["Supervised","Unsupervised","Reinforcement"],"reponse":"Supervised","explication":"The user’s “spam” click is a label: each example carries the right answer.","notes":"Likely wrong answers: “unsupervised”, because nobody annotated the emails on purpose, or “reinforcement”, because the filter improves with use. Yet each marked email carries the expected answer: that is exactly a label. Sorting emails into “spam” and “not spam” is the textbook example of supervised classification."},{"type":"etapes","titre":"The decisive question","etapes":[{"titre":"Do the examples carry the answer?","texte":"The question to ask for each situation, about the training examples"},{"titre":"Yes: supervised","texte":"Classification if the answer is a category, regression if it is a number"},{"titre":"No: unsupervised","texte":"The algorithm forms groups that the researcher must interpret"},{"titre":"Rewards: reinforcement","texte":"The program learns by acting and receiving rewards or penalties"}],"notes":"10 min of sharing results. Write the question on the board: “Do the training examples carry the right answer?” It settles almost every case. Careful: look at the examples used for training, not at the new cases, which never have a label."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Test set","traduction":"jeu de test","definition":"The part of the data set aside to evaluate the model on examples it has never seen.","exemple":"Like an exam made of questions the student has never seen.","notes":"A rule to have students write down: a model is never evaluated on the examples used to train it. The data are split in two: the training set (jeu d’entraînement) fits the model; the test set, set aside, measures its performance at the end. The goal is generalization: handling new cases well."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Overfitting","traduction":"sur-apprentissage","definition":"A flaw of a model that memorizes the details of the training set and generalizes poorly to new data.","exemple":"Almost perfect in training, poor in the test: like the student who memorized the solutions without understanding the course.","notes":"The warning sign: a large gap between the training score and the test score. In the lesson’s example, a model that is too complex learns the training poems by heart, with their accidental details, instead of grasping what characterizes each period."},{"type":"question","titre":"Another trap: “enfans”","question":"All your Romantic poems come from old editions that write “enfans” for “enfants”. What does the model learn?","reponse":"The spelling of the edition, rather than the style of the period.","explication":"A model relies on anything that distinguishes the examples, even an accidental detail: a good score does not guarantee that it has learned what you are looking for.","notes":"An example taken from the lesson. Let students think for a minute before showing the answer. A point to remember for literature students: knowing the origin of every text in the corpus (edition, spelling, digitization) is part of the method."},{"type":"section","numero":2,"titre":"Tutorial exercises","sousTitre":"Exercises 1 to 3 in class, exercise 4 as homework","notes":"The exercises are on the site, in the chapter 1 tutorial (TD). Ask students not to open “Show the solution” before the class correction."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Exercises 1 and 2","duree":25,"consignes":["Exercise 1: for supervised and unsupervised learning, a definition, the data, the tasks, one difficulty","Exercise 2: a comparison table of the two types of learning, written from memory","Alone first, then in pairs","Class correction"],"notes":"Alone, then in pairs, then correction. The table in exercise 2 is written without notes: this is exactly what the exam asks for. If a pair finishes early, ask them where to place semi-supervised learning and self-supervised learning."},{"type":"deux","titre":"Correcting exercise 1","colonnes":[{"titre":"Supervised","points":["Data: examples with their **label** (an email and “spam”)","Tasks: classification, regression","Evaluation: comparing with the labels of the test set","Difficulty: annotating is costly; labels reflect the choices, sometimes the prejudices, of annotators"]},{"titre":"Unsupervised","points":["Data: examples **without labels**","Tasks: clustering, dimensionality reduction, anomaly detection, topic modeling","Evaluation: coherence of the groups, usefulness for interpretation","Difficulty: naming and interpreting the groups obtained"]}],"notes":"Add the in-between cases orally. Semi-supervised learning combines a few labeled examples with many raw examples. Self-supervised learning takes the label from the data itself, for example by hiding the next word of a sentence: this is how large language models are pre-trained. Reinforcement learning has no corrected examples: an agent receives rewards or penalties and learns the strategy that maximizes them."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Correcting exercise 2","entetes":["Criterion","Supervised","Unsupervised"],"lignes":[["Data","Examples with their labels","Examples only"],["Question asked","Which category? What value?","What groups, what structures?"],["Typical tasks","Classification, regression","Clustering, dimensionality reduction, topics"],["Algorithms","Regressions, k-nearest neighbors, trees, networks","k-means, hierarchical clustering, PCA, LDA"],["Evaluation","Direct: labels of the test set","Indirect: coherence of the groups, human reading"],["Human work","Annotating before learning","Interpreting after learning"],["Example in SHS","Attributing an anonymous text to a known author","Grouping novels by their vocabulary"]],"legende":"In supervised learning, we learn to reproduce known answers; in unsupervised learning, we look for an organization that we do not know yet.","notes":"Have students compare row by row with the pairs’ tables. Details of the “Algorithms” row: linear and logistic regression, k-nearest neighbors, decision trees, neural networks; for unsupervised learning, principal component analysis (PCA) and LDA, for topic modeling. For evaluation: accuracy or mean error on one side, silhouette score on the other. Have students copy the summary sentence."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Exercise 3: six examples of learning","duree":15,"consignes":["In pairs, find six examples of learning","For each one: the type of learning, the task, the data used","At least two examples from the humanities or from literature"],"notes":"Above all, check that the type and the data are consistent: if labels are needed, the learning is supervised. Encourage examples taken from the course: poems, novels from the Maghreb, political speeches, messages in Darja, surveys on reading."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Correcting exercise 3: one possible answer","entetes":["Example","Type","Task","Data"],"lignes":[["Sorting emails into “spam” and “not spam”","Supervised","Classification","Emails marked by users"],["Attributing a disputed text to an author (stylometry)","Supervised","Classification","Texts whose author is known"],["Estimating when a poem was written","Supervised","Regression","Poems whose date is known"],["Grouping novels by their vocabulary","Unsupervised","Clustering","Texts only"],["Respondent profiles in a survey on reading","Unsupervised","Clustering","Answers without labels"],["Learning Go by playing against oneself","Reinforcement","Successive actions","Games; winning serves as the reward"]],"legende":"Other answers are possible: check that the type is consistent with the data.","notes":"The SHS examples: stylometry, which Mosteller and Wallace applied in 1964 to some of the *Federalist Papers*; the date of a poem; reader profiles. Go refers to AlphaGo Zero (DeepMind, 2017). Have each pair read out one example: the class says whether the type and the data match."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Launching exercise 4","duree":15,"consignes":["Can today’s computers do these six tasks? One task per pair","Play ping-pong properly · drive a car in a city center · discover and prove theorems","Write a funny story · give a relevant legal opinion · perform a complex surgical operation","Define the success criterion: compared with whom, under what conditions?","Look for primary, dated sources","A 3-minute report at the next session"],"notes":"Share out the six tasks, even if it means giving the same task to several pairs. Stress the words in the instructions: “properly”, “relevant”, “funny”; defining these criteria is part of the work. Do not show the solution on the site: it is a state of knowledge from September 2026, in a field that changes very fast. Students will have to check what has changed since then."},{"type":"etapes","titre":"Method: carrying out the research","progressif":true,"etapes":[{"titre":"Define the criterion","texte":"Compared with a beginner, a professional, the best human? Under what conditions?"},{"titre":"Go back to primary sources","texte":"Peer-reviewed articles, technical reports; preprints (arXiv, HAL) with caution"},{"titre":"Demo, product or announcement?","texte":"A company presenting its own product is not a neutral source"},{"titre":"Examine the conditions","texte":"Number of trials, environment, opponents, share of human supervision, failure rate"},{"titre":"Date and cross-check","texte":"Date of the result, date you consulted it; at least two independent sources"},{"titre":"A nuanced verdict","texte":"“Yes, but…”, “under controlled conditions”, “not yet”"}],"notes":"This method applies to any documentary research on AI. Preprints are useful but have not always been peer-reviewed; Google Scholar helps you find articles. To cross-check, a general overview such as the book by Jean-Gabriel Ganascia (*L’IA expliquée aux humains*, 2024), cited in the course references, can serve as a second source. Nuances are the expected answer, not an admission of weakness."},{"type":"points","titre":"Common mistakes to correct","points":["“Unsupervised” does not mean “without humans”: the researcher chooses, then interprets","The new cases have no label: that is no reason to conclude it is unsupervised","**Classification**: known categories; **clustering**: discovered groups"],"notes":"For each mistake, have students find the card that shows it. First mistake: the researcher chooses the data and the number of groups, then interprets; the 200 novels do not sort themselves into “families”. Second: the 3,000 speeches. Third: dating a poem using 600 dated poems (classification) versus dividing 200 novels into stylistic families (clustering)."},{"type":"fin","titre":"Homework","points":[{"texte":"**Prepare exercise 4**: a 3-minute report","sous":["The site’s solution dates from September 2026: check what has changed since then"]},"**Reread** “AI approaches to problem solving” (chapter 1)","**Learn** the glossary, in French and in English"],"texte":"Next session: first Python lab session (TP)","notes":"Before ending the class, check the numerical objective with a show of hands: who classified at least 8 cards out of 10? The glossary is learned in pairs: one French → English card and one English → French card, with the definition on the back."}]},{"id":"seance-4","numero":4,"titre":"First Python lab (TP): variables, lists and a poem’s words","resume":"Open a notebook, create variables and a list, then count the words of a stanza by Verlaine and explain the difference caused by punctuation.","duree":"1 h 30 min","lien":"cours.html#c2-fondamentaux","fiche":"enseignant.html#guide-seance-4","diapos":[{"type":"titre","surtitre":"Chapter 2 · Session 4 · Lab session (TP)","titre":"First Python lab session","sousTitre":"A notebook, variables, a list and the words of a poem by Verlaine","notes":"Before the session: check that the Google accounts have been created (Colab) or that Jupyter opens, for example with run.bat notebook. Prepare a notebook with the headings of the steps and enlarge the font: you will type the code live. One computer for two is enough; no programming prerequisites are required."},{"type":"points","titre":"Session objectives","points":["Open a **notebook**, run and save a cell","Create **variables** and a **list**","Count the words of a stanza by Verlaine","Explain the gap between “items” and “words”: punctuation"],"notes":"Read the objectives aloud and reassure the students: nobody is expected to know how to program already. Announce the rule of the day: we predict the output before running the code, and we read error messages instead of fearing them. By the end of the session, everyone should be able to say what split() does and what NameError means."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Forming the pairs","duree":10,"consignes":["One computer for two: the **driver** types, the **navigator** reads the instructions, checks and suggests","The less confident student starts at the keyboard; the navigator never takes it over","Swap roles every 15 minutes, at the signal","Default environment: Google Colab, in the browser"],"materiel":"Computers, a connection, Google accounts (Colab) or Jupyter installed","notes":"Form the pairs based on the questionnaire: mix levels and equipment, and seat students without a computer with a classmate who has one. Colab needs nothing to be installed: a Google account is enough. You are the one who gives the signal to swap roles."},{"type":"section","numero":1,"titre":"First notebook","sousTitre":"One cell, Shift + Enter, one result","notes":"15 min. Goal: every pair sees its first result appear."},{"type":"etapes","titre":"Opening and using a notebook","progressif":true,"etapes":[{"titre":"Open Colab","texte":"colab.research.google.com, then create a notebook"},{"titre":"Type the code","texte":"In a cell, without copying and pasting"},{"titre":"Run it","texte":"Shift + Enter: the result appears below the cell"},{"titre":"Save it","texte":"In Google Drive: the files in the session are deleted when it closes"}],"notes":"A notebook mixes code cells, results and text. With Jupyter, launch run.bat notebook or type jupyter notebook in the terminal: a tab opens in the browser. Show each action on the projector, then walk round the room until everyone sees their result."},{"type":"code","titre":"The first cell","code":"# A comment: Python ignores this line\nprint(\"Bonjour, Médéa !\")\nprint(\"Kateb Yacine est né en\", 1929)","sortie":"Bonjour, Médéa !\nKateb Yacine est né en 1929","legende":"`print()` displays the values you give it, separated by a space.","notes":"Type live while saying what you are doing, then press Shift + Enter. Point out the straight quotation marks, the parentheses, and the comma that separates the text from the number 1929. Everything after # is a comment, meant for the human reader."},{"type":"section","numero":2,"titre":"Variables and lists","sousTitre":"Storing values under a name","notes":"30 min: variables and types (15 min), then lists (15 min)."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Variable","traduction":"variable","definition":"A name that refers to a value in memory. The = sign means “receives”, not “is equal to”.","exemple":"`annee = 1956` stores 1956 under the name `annee`.","notes":"Insist on the meaning of =: “annee receives 1956”. Each value has a type: int (whole number), float (decimal number), str (character string, in quotation marks), bool (True or False). A variable name contains no spaces and, preferably, no accents: words are joined with an underscore, as in mots_par_page."},{"type":"code","titre":"Variables, types and f-strings","code":"titre = \"Nedjma\"   # str: character string\nannee = 1956       # int: whole number\nprint(type(titre), type(annee))\nprint(f\"{titre} de Kateb Yacine paraît en {annee}.\")","sortie":"<class 'str'> <class 'int'>\nNedjma de Kateb Yacine paraît en 1956.","legende":"In an **f-string**, each expression in curly braces is replaced by its value.","notes":"Have students predict each line before running it. type() gives the type of a value: str for text, int for a whole number. Question to ask: what happens if you forget the f before the quotation marks? Expected: the curly braces are displayed as they are, {titre} instead of Nedjma."},{"type":"code","titre":"A deliberate error","code":"titre = \"Nedjma","sortie":"SyntaxError: unterminated string literal (detected at line 1)","legende":"The last line of the message: the type of error, then the explanation (an unterminated string).","notes":"Leave out the closing quotation mark on purpose and run the cell. The message starts with the location of the error, with a ^ under the faulty spot; read the last line first, aloud: “unterminated string literal, detected at line 1”. Correct it and run it again. The message to get across: an error is information, not a failure."},{"type":"question","titre":"Predict before running","question":"`auteurs = [\"Hugo\", \"Baudelaire\", \"Maupassant\"]`: what is the value of `auteurs[0]`?","choix":["Hugo","Baudelaire","Maupassant","An error"],"reponse":"Hugo: indices start at 0.","explication":"`auteurs[0]` is the first item, `auteurs[1]` the second, `auteurs[-1]` the last.","notes":"Have students vote by a show of hands before revealing the answer. Many will answer Baudelaire: this is the common mistake of looking for the first item at [1]. Then run the code to settle it."},{"type":"code","titre":"Lists","code":"auteurs = [\"Hugo\", \"Baudelaire\", \"Maupassant\"]\nauteurs.append(\"Kateb Yacine\")       # add at the end\nprint(auteurs[0], \"|\", auteurs[-1])  # first and last\nprint(len(auteurs), \"auteurs\")\nauteurs.sort()                       # sort in place\nprint(auteurs)\nprint(auteurs[1:3])                  # positions 1 and 2","sortie":"Hugo | Kateb Yacine\n4 auteurs\n['Baudelaire', 'Hugo', 'Kateb Yacine', 'Maupassant']\n['Hugo', 'Kateb Yacine']","legende":"`append` adds at the end, `sort` sorts in place, `len` counts the items.","notes":"Run the code, having students predict each line. auteurs[-1] refers to the last item. The slice auteurs[1:3] takes positions 1 and 2: the right-hand bound is excluded. sort() changes the list itself, in alphabetical order."},{"type":"section","numero":3,"titre":"Counting the words of a poem","sousTitre":"Verlaine, “Il pleure dans mon cœur” (1874)","notes":"25 min, including the time to type the program."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Counting the words of a stanza","duree":25,"consignes":["Type the program `compter_mots.py`, without copying and pasting","Run it and write down the two lines it displays","Explain the gap: why 21 items for 19 words?","Fastest pairs: find the most frequent word (tutorial exercise 3)"],"materiel":"The program on the projector; the chapter 2 tutorial (TD) on the website","notes":"Driver and navigator swap roles halfway through. For the fastest pairs, expected: three words tie, il, mon and cœur, twice each; Counter(mots).most_common(3), from the collections module, gives [('il', 2), ('mon', 2), ('cœur', 2)]. A reader’s remark to share: “pleure” and “pleut” are counted as two unrelated words; the echo of sounds that makes the poem escapes the count."},{"type":"code","titre":"compter_mots.py","code":"poeme = \"\"\"Il pleure dans mon cœur\nComme il pleut sur la ville ;\nQuelle est cette langueur\nQui pénètre mon cœur ?\"\"\"\n\nmots = poeme.lower().split()   # lowercase, then split\nprint(len(mots), \"éléments\")   # \";\" and \"?\" count too\nmots = [m for m in mots if m.isalpha()]  # letters only\nprint(len(mots), \"mots,\", len(set(mots)), \"mots différents\")","sortie":"21 éléments\n19 mots, 16 mots différents","legende":"Paul Verlaine, “Il pleure dans mon cœur”, *Romances sans paroles* (1874), first stanza.","notes":"Triple quotation marks allow a text over several lines. lower() converts to lowercase, split() cuts at spaces and returns a list. [m for m in mots if m.isalpha()] is a list comprehension: “the m in mots that contain only letters”. set() removes duplicates: its length gives the number of different words."},{"type":"question","titre":"Explaining the gap","question":"Why does the program find 21 items for 19 words?","reponse":"`split()` cuts at spaces: “;” and “?”, preceded by a space in French, become two extra “words”.","explication":"`isalpha()` keeps only the items made of letters: the punctuation disappears, leaving 19 words.","notes":"Let them search for two or three minutes. Possible hint: display part of the list with print(poeme.lower().split()[8:12]), which gives ['sur', 'la', 'ville', ';']. French typography puts a space before “;” and “:”, and split() counts them as words. Comparing texts requires defining what a “word” is before counting."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Common mistakes","entetes":["Symptom","Cause","Remedy"],"lignes":[["`SyntaxError`","Code copied from Word or a PDF: curly quotation marks “ ”","Retype the code with straight quotation marks \" \""],["`NameError`","Cells run in the wrong order","Run the cells again in order, from top to bottom"],["Wrong item","First item looked for at `[1]`","The first item is `[0]`"]],"legende":"A reflex to acquire: read the last line of the error message first.","notes":"If time allows, show the first one live: print(“Bonjour, Médéa !”) with curly quotation marks gives SyntaxError: invalid character '“' (U+201C). For the second: a cell that uses mots, run before the cell that creates mots, gives NameError: name 'mots' is not defined."},{"type":"question","titre":"Wrap-up","question":"What does `split()` do?","reponse":"It cuts a string at spaces and returns a **list** of words.","explication":"`\"Il pleure dans mon cœur\".split()` gives `['Il', 'pleure', 'dans', 'mon', 'cœur']`.","notes":"Wrap-up, 10 min in all. Draw lots for the pair that answers: everyone must be ready. Remind students that split() does not change the string: it returns a new list."},{"type":"question","titre":"Wrap-up","question":"What does `NameError: name 'mots' is not defined` mean?","reponse":"Python does not know the name `mots`: the variable has not been created (yet).","explication":"In a notebook, the cell that creates `mots` has not been run, or the cells were run in the wrong order. Another cause: a typo in the name.","notes":"Link this to the common mistake of the session. Remedy: run the cells again in order, from top to bottom."},{"type":"fin","titre":"Before you leave","points":["Save your notebook (in Google Drive with Colab)","Retype the examples from “The fundamentals of the language”, predicting each output","Do tutorial exercise 1: palindromes","Code for twenty minutes, three times during the week"],"texte":"Next session: querying a corpus with pandas.","notes":"Check that each pair has saved its notebook before leaving the room: the files in a Colab session are deleted when it closes. The examples to retype are in the lesson “The fundamentals of the language” in chapter 2; tutorial exercise 1 has a solution online, to be opened only after working on it."}]},{"id":"seance-5","numero":5,"titre":"Lab session (TP): querying a corpus with pandas","resume":"Load a CSV file, filter it, sort it and group it to answer a question asked in plain language, then draw a chart with a title and interpret it with caution.","duree":"1 h 30 min","lien":"cours.html#c2-numpy-pandas","fiche":"enseignant.html#guide-seance-5","diapos":[{"type":"titre","surtitre":"Chapter 2 · Session 5 · Lab session (TP)","titre":"Querying a corpus with pandas","sousTitre":"Ten works, six columns, questions asked in plain language","notes":"Before the session, put oeuvres.csv, the file from the pandas lesson, where the students will work: in the notebook’s folder with Jupyter, otherwise in the Colab session. Same pairs as in session 4, with driver and navigator swapping roles every fifteen minutes."},{"type":"points","titre":"Session objectives","points":["Load a **CSV** file and describe it","**Filter**, **sort** and **group** to answer a question asked in plain language","Draw a chart with a title and **interpret it with caution**"],"notes":"Announce the thread of the session: a question in plain language first, the code afterwards. Remind students right away that the word counts in the corpus are rounded values invented for the exercise, not real counts."},{"type":"code","titre":"Review: predict before running","code":"vers = \"Il pleure dans mon cœur\"\nmots = vers.split()\nprint(len(mots), mots[0], mots[-1])","legende":"Write on paper what the last line will display, then run it.","notes":"10 min. Everyone writes their prediction on paper, alone, before running anything. The pairs then type the three lines and compare. The answer is on the next slide."},{"type":"question","titre":"Review: the answer","question":"What does `print(len(mots), mots[0], mots[-1])` display?","reponse":"`5 Il cœur`","explication":"`split()` gives 5 words; index 0 refers to the first, index -1 to the last.","notes":"Expected mistakes: “pleure” for mots[0], if indices are counted from 1, or an answer in square brackets. Have students justify each of the three values displayed."},{"type":"section","numero":1,"titre":"Reading and describing the corpus","sousTitre":"From spreadsheet to DataFrame","notes":"15 min."},{"type":"definition","terme":"DataFrame","traduction":"DataFrame","definition":"A pandas table made of columns (**Series**) that share the same index: the equivalent of a spreadsheet.","exemple":"One row = one work; one column = one variable (title, author, year, genre, language, number of words).","notes":"First open oeuvres.csv in a spreadsheet: it is a simple text file, one line per work, with columns separated by commas (CSV, comma-separated values). The DataFrame is the same table, in Python. The index is the labels of the rows: 0, 1, 2… by default."},{"type":"code","titre":"Reading the CSV file","code":"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"oeuvres.csv\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\nprint(df.shape)                     # (rows, columns)\nprint(df[[\"titre\", \"annee\"]].head(4))","sortie":"(10, 6)\n               titre  annee\n0     Les Misérables   1862\n1  Les Fleurs du mal   1857\n2            Bel-Ami   1885\n3   La Grande Maison   1952","legende":"`shape` gives the dimensions, `head()` the first rows (5 by default).","notes":"import pandas as pd loads the library under its usual alias. Get into the habit of writing encoding=\"utf-8\", as for every file in the course. In a notebook, df.head() on its own as the last line of a cell displays a readable table of the first 5 rows and the 6 columns. If Python answers FileNotFoundError, the file is not in the notebook’s folder or in the Colab session."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"What info() reveals","entetes":["Column","Type (Dtype)","Non-empty values"],"lignes":[["titre","str","10"],["auteur","str","10"],["annee","int64","10"],["genre","str","10"],["langue","str","10"],["nb_mots","int64","10"]],"legende":"Output of `df.info()` with pandas 3; with pandas 2, text appears as `object` instead of `str`.","notes":"Have students run df.info(): the command lists the columns, their type and the number of non-empty values. Here it is 10 everywhere: no missing data. int64 refers to whole numbers, str to text. Remind them again that the word counts are fictitious: we are learning a method, not facts about these works."},{"type":"section","numero":2,"titre":"Querying the corpus","sousTitre":"Filter, sort, group, plot","notes":"20 min to filter and sort, 20 min to group, 15 min for the chart."},{"type":"question","titre":"First the question, in plain language","question":"Which works in the corpus are in Arabic and were published after 1950?","reponse":"*Bayn al-Qasrayn* (1956) and *Awraq al-Zaytun* (1964).","explication":"*Al-Ayyam* (1929) is indeed in Arabic, but was published before 1950: **two** conditions are needed at once.","notes":"First have students answer by hand, looking at the projected table: the question guides the code, not the other way round. Then ask which columns to query. Expected: langue and annee."},{"type":"code","titre":"Turning the question into a filter","code":"choix = df[(df[\"langue\"] == \"arabe\") & (df[\"annee\"] > 1950)]\nprint(choix[[\"titre\", \"annee\"]])","sortie":"             titre  annee\n6  Bayn al-Qasrayn   1956\n7  Awraq al-Zaytun   1964","legende":"Each condition in parentheses; `&` means “and”, `|` means “or”.","notes":"Each condition produces a column of True and False; df[condition] keeps the rows that are true. The original index (6, 7) is kept. A common trap: and instead of &, which causes ValueError: The truth value of a Series is ambiguous… Forgetting the parentheses around each condition also causes an error."},{"type":"code","titre":"Sorting with sort_values()","code":"plus_longs = df.sort_values(\"nb_mots\", ascending=False)\nprint(plus_longs[[\"titre\", \"nb_mots\"]].head(3))","sortie":"             titre  nb_mots\n0   Les Misérables   530000\n6  Bayn al-Qasrayn   160000\n2          Bel-Ami   125000","legende":"`ascending=False`: descending order, from the longest to the shortest.","notes":"The question in plain language first: “which are the three longest works?”. Without ascending=False, the sort is ascending. For the fastest pairs: sort the result of the previous filter by year, with choix.sort_values(\"annee\")."},{"type":"code","titre":"Grouping with groupby()","code":"# Average number of words for each genre, rounded\nprint(df.groupby(\"genre\")[\"nb_mots\"].mean().round())","sortie":"genre\npoésie     28333.0\nroman     174500.0\nrécit      48000.0\nName: nb_mots, dtype: float64","legende":"`groupby` splits the rows by genre, then calculates the mean of each group.","notes":"Read the line like a sentence: “group by genre, take the nb_mots column, calculate its mean, round it”. “récit” is placed after “roman”: Python sorts strings by the Unicode number of their characters, and “é” comes after all the letters without accents. Without round(), poésie would display 28333.333333."},{"type":"code","titre":"Counting with value_counts()","code":"print(df[\"genre\"].value_counts())   # counts per genre","sortie":"genre\nroman     6\npoésie    3\nrécit     1\nName: count, dtype: int64","legende":"The count of each value, from the most frequent to the rarest.","notes":"value_counts() counts the occurrences of each value. The same move on language: df[\"langue\"].value_counts() gives français 5, arabe 3, anglais 2. Point out the 1 for récit: it sets up the next question."},{"type":"question","titre":"Interpreting with caution","question":"The genre “récit” has an average of 48,000 words. What is this figure worth?","reponse":"Not much: it rests on **a single** text, *Al-Ayyam*.","explication":"An exact calculation does not make a solid conclusion: before a mean, check how many texts it summarizes.","notes":"Let the pairs discuss for a minute. Going further: three poetry collections are no more enough to speak of “poetry” in general. Ten works chosen for an exercise do not represent any literature."},{"type":"chiffre","valeur":"530,000","legende":"words for *Les Misérables* (fictitious value): the corpus mean rises to 118,000 words, while the median stays at 68,500.","source":"Chapter 2 exercise corpus (invented word counts)","notes":"Have students calculate: df[\"nb_mots\"].mean() gives 118000.0, df[\"nb_mots\"].median() gives 68500.0. The median splits the series into two halves; a single very long novel pulls the mean up. The same effect on the novels: 174,500 words on average with Les Misérables, 103,400 without it, that is (1,047,000 − 530,000) / 5. To remember: when texts have unequal lengths, always look at the median."},{"type":"code","titre":"A bar chart","code":"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nmoyennes = df.groupby(\"genre\")[\"nb_mots\"].mean()\nmoyennes.plot(kind=\"bar\")            # a bar chart\nplt.title(\"Longueur moyenne des œuvres par genre\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Genre\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Nombre de mots (valeurs fictives)\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig(\"mots_par_genre.png\")    # save the image\nplt.show()                           # display it","legende":"Three bars, one per genre (poésie, roman, récit): the bar for the novel is clearly the tallest.","notes":"15 min. The title and axis labels are not decoration: without them, the chart cannot be interpreted. savefig() saves a PNG image that can be inserted into a thesis; have students find the file in the notebook’s folder or the Colab session. Ask: what does the bar for the novel show, and what does it hide? Expected: the weight of Les Misérables."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Wrap-up: “In this small corpus…”","duree":10,"consignes":["Each pair writes a sentence that begins with “In this small corpus…”","The sentence relies on a displayed result: filter, mean, count or chart","It does not generalize beyond the ten works","Then start tutorial exercise 4: filter, group and sort eight texts"],"materiel":"The chapter 2 tutorial (TD), exercise 4, on the website","notes":"Have two or three sentences read aloud and correct the generalizations: ten works and fictitious numbers allow no general conclusion. For you, the answers to exercise 4: novels of more than 70,000 words, T2, T3 and T8; by language, English 2 texts and 46,750 words on average, Arabic 3 and 24,067, French 3 and 52,300; the three shortest, T5, T1 and T7."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Common mistakes","entetes":["Symptom","Cause","Remedy"],"lignes":[["`FileNotFoundError`","The CSV is neither in the notebook’s folder nor in the Colab session","Put the file in the right place, check its name"],["Only one column","CSV saved by an Excel set to French","`pd.read_csv(\"oeuvres.csv\", sep=\";\")`"],["`KeyError`","`df[\"genre\", \"titre\"]`","`df[[\"genre\", \"titre\"]]`: double brackets"],["`ValueError`","`and` between two conditions","`&`, each condition in parentheses"]],"notes":"An Excel set to French often separates columns with semicolons: shape then displays (10, 1) instead of (10, 6). One column is selected with single brackets, several columns with a list of names, hence the double brackets."},{"type":"fin","titre":"Homework","points":["Finish tutorial exercise 4 after twenty minutes of working on it without the solution","Add three works to the CSV and note what changes in the results"],"texte":"Next session: chapter 3 tutorial (TD), expert system and forward chaining.","notes":"For the three added works, advise students to watch the mean and the median above all: a single very long or very short text is enough to move them. The solution to exercise 4 is online, to be opened only after working on it."}]},{"id":"seance-6","numero":6,"titre":"Tutorial (TD): expert system and forward chaining","resume":"Name the three components of an expert system, draw the graph of the tutorial’s rules and work through forward chaining, explaining why R4 and R7 do not fire.","duree":"1 h 30 min","lien":"cours.html#c3-symbolique","fiche":"enseignant.html#guide-seance-6","diapos":[{"type":"titre","surtitre":"Chapter 3 · Session 6 · Tutorial (TD)","titre":"Expert system and forward chaining","sousTitre":"From the rules of versification to tutorial exercise 1","notes":"Materials: the seven A5 rule cards for the role play, the text of tutorial exercise 1, the projector for the demonstration. Divide the board into two columns: “fact base” and “fired rules”. Prerequisite: the lesson “Symbolic AI: expert systems”."},{"type":"points","titre":"Session objectives","points":["Name the **three components** of an expert system","Draw the **rule graph** of tutorial exercise 1","Work through **forward chaining**, step by step","Explain why **R4** and **R7** do not fire"],"notes":"Announce the path: first we act out the reasoning with rules about poetry, then we formalize it on the tutorial exercise, where the facts are simple letters."},{"type":"question","titre":"Hook","question":"How do you recognize a sonnet?","reponse":"IF the poem has 14 lines AND 2 quatrains AND 2 tercets, THEN it is a sonnet.","explication":"Each answer written in the form IF … THEN … is a **rule**: together, they form a **rule base**.","notes":"10 min. Write the answers on the board, then rewrite them with the class in the form IF … THEN. Also expected: 14 lines, 3 quatrains AND 1 couplet for the English sonnet, also called the Shakespearean sonnet. A treatise on versification that states “a sonnet has fourteen lines, divided into two quatrains and two tercets” is already stating a rule."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Expert system","traduction":"système expert","definition":"A symbolic AI program that reproduces the reasoning of a specialist in a narrow field, using facts and rules that a human can read.","exemple":"MYCIN (Stanford, 1970s) diagnosed bacterial blood infections and proposed an antibiotic.","notes":"Symbolic AI represents knowledge with symbols that a human can read, facts and rules, rather than with numbers learned from data. DENDRAL (Stanford, from 1965) is often regarded as the first expert system; MYCIN was never used routinely in hospitals. The strength of the approach: explainability, since each conclusion is justified by the exact chain of rules that fired."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"The three components","entetes":["Component","Role","Example"],"lignes":[["**Fact base**","What we know; it grows as the reasoning goes on","“the poem has 14 lines”"],["**Rule base**","The knowledge: IF premises THEN conclusion","IF 14 lines AND 2 quatrains AND 2 tercets THEN sonnet"],["**Inference engine**","Finds the applicable rules, fires them, adds their conclusions","The same for medicine or poetry"]],"notes":"The inference engine does not depend on the field: you move from medicine to poetry by changing only the rules. The premises of a rule are joined by AND, also written ∩ in the text of the tutorial; we will write ∧."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"The rules for poetic forms","entetes":["Rule","IF (premises)","THEN (conclusion)"],"lignes":[["R1","3 lines AND 5-7-5 syllables","haiku"],["R2","14 lines AND 2 quatrains AND 2 tercets","sonnet"],["R3","14 lines AND 3 quatrains AND 1 couplet","sonnet"],["R4","sonnet AND 2 tercets","Italian or French sonnet"],["R5","sonnet AND 1 couplet","English sonnet"],["R6","sonnet","fixed form"],["R7","haiku","fixed form"]],"legende":"In order of priority. The observations (“14 lines”, “2 tercets”…) are non-deducible facts.","notes":"Hand out the seven rule cards. The Italian or French sonnet (Petrarch, Ronsard, Baudelaire) has two quatrains and two tercets; the English sonnet, also called the Shakespearean sonnet, has three quatrains and a couplet. Point out that R4 to R7 use facts that other rules must first deduce."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Role play: the inference engine","duree":20,"consignes":["Seven students are the rules R1 to R7; another plays the engine, another the secretary","On the board, the initial base: 14 lines, 2 quatrains, 2 tercets, alexandrines","The engine calls the rules in order, starting from R1","A rule fires if all its premises are on the board and its conclusion is new","The secretary writes the conclusion, the rule sits down, and we start again from R1","A complete pass with no rule firing ends the game"],"materiel":"One A5 card per rule; the board divided into “fact base” and “fired rules”","notes":"Timing: roles 3 min, facts 2 min, cycles 10 min, then 5 min for two other cases: a haiku (R1 then R7) and a sonnet printed as a single block, which fires nothing. The initial base is that of Du Bellay’s sonnet “Heureux qui, comme Ulysse, a fait un beau voyage” (Les Regrets, 1558). Debriefing: “Who knew it was a sonnet?” Nobody: each rule only looked at the board."},{"type":"etapes","titre":"Expected: Du Bellay’s sonnet","progressif":true,"etapes":[{"titre":"Initial base","texte":"14 lines, 2 quatrains, 2 tercets, alexandrines"},{"titre":"Cycle 1","texte":"R1 fails (no “3 lines”); R2 applies: we add “sonnet”"},{"titre":"Cycle 2","texte":"We start again from R1; R4 applies: “Italian or French sonnet”"},{"titre":"Cycle 3","texte":"R5 fails (no “1 couplet”); R6 adds “fixed form”"},{"titre":"Cycle 4: stop","texte":"No rule produces a new fact any more"}],"notes":"Rules fired: R2, R4, R6; three facts deduced. No rule uses “alexandrines”: this fact stays inactive. What to take away: the engine compares symbols without understanding them; the order of the rules changes the path; outside the cases it was designed for, the system fails."},{"type":"deux","titre":"Forward or backward chaining?","colonnes":[{"titre":"Forward chaining","points":["**Data**-driven: from the facts to the goal","“What can I deduce?”","Drawback: it can deduce many useless facts"]},{"titre":"Backward chaining","points":["**Goal**-driven: from the goal to the facts","“What do I need to prove this?”","Advantage: only the rules useful for the goal are examined; well suited to diagnosis"]}],"notes":"Mini-lecture: 10 min with the next slide. An example of backward chaining: the hypothesis “English sonnet?” requires, by R5, “sonnet” and “1 couplet”. This last fact is non-deducible and missing from the base: the hypothesis is rejected at once. French terms: chaînage avant, chaînage arrière."},{"type":"points","titre":"Rule order and non-deducible facts","points":["**Conflict resolution**: several rules are applicable, the engine must choose one","The tutorial’s strategy: a **rule order** fixed in advance","A rule that has already fired does not fire a second time","**Non-deducible fact**: it is not the conclusion of any rule","Only observation or the user can provide it: H, G and K in the tutorial"],"notes":"In the lesson, the engine fires the first applicable rule in order, then starts again from the top of the list, as in the role play. The tutorial’s solution goes through the rules in the required order without going back to the top: the result is the same. In backward chaining, a non-deducible sub-goal that is missing from the base makes that path fail, or leads the system to ask the user."},{"type":"section","titre":"Tutorial exercise 1","sousTitre":"Seven rules, initial base {H, K}, goal C","notes":"35 min: question a (15 min), then question b (20 min)."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"The exercise: seven rules","entetes":["Rule","IF","THEN"],"lignes":[["R1","A","E"],["R2","B","D"],["R3","H","A ∧ F"],["R4","E ∧ G","C"],["R5","E ∧ K","B"],["R6","D ∧ E ∧ K","C"],["R7","G ∧ K ∧ F","A"]],"legende":"Order: R3 R7 R1 R4 R5 R2 R6 · Non-deducible: H, G, K · Initial base: {H, K} · Goal: C","notes":"In the text of the syllabus, conjunction is written ∩: here this symbol means “AND”, which we write ∧. “E ∧ G → C” reads: if E and G are established, then C is established. Have students read the order aloud: it does not follow the numbering."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Question a: drawing the graph","duree":15,"consignes":["One **rectangle** per fact, one **circle** per rule","An arrow from each condition to the rule, then from the rule to each conclusion","Arrows that enter the same rule: conditions joined by AND","Two rules that lead to the same fact (R3 and R7 for A, R4 and R6 for C): OR","Find the facts that no arrow points to"],"notes":"This is called an AND/OR graph. Individual or pair work, then correction on the board. A layout tip taken from the solution: place H below R3 so that no arrow crosses another. The corrected graph is in the online tutorial."},{"type":"question","titre":"Question a: a first diagnosis","question":"Which facts does no arrow point to? What can you conclude?","reponse":"G, H and K: these are the non-deducible facts. G is not in the initial base: R4 and R7 can never fire.","explication":"The only possible path to C therefore goes through R6.","notes":"Let the students formulate the conclusion themselves. H and K are provided by the initial base; G is missing, and no rule can produce it. This is the key to the whole exercise."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Question b: working through forward chaining","duree":20,"consignes":["Examine the rules in the required order: R3, R7, R1, R4, R5, R2, R6","A rule fires if **all** its conditions are in the base","Add **all** its conclusions to the fact base","A rule that has already fired is not run again","Stop as soon as C is in the base, or when a pass adds nothing new"],"materiel":"One grid per student: step, rule examined, condition, fires?, fact base afterwards","notes":"Each student fills in their grid alone, step by step; the correction is then done on the board. Walk round and watch for: R7 fired although G is missing, F forgotten after R3, a rule run again, the numbering order followed instead of the required order."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Question b: the solution","entetes":["Step","Rule","Condition","Fires?","Fact base afterwards"],"lignes":[["1","R3","H","Yes: H is in the base","H, K, A, F"],["2","R7","G ∧ K ∧ F","No: G is absent","H, K, A, F"],["3","R1","A","Yes","H, K, A, F, E"],["4","R4","E ∧ G","No: G is absent","H, K, A, F, E"],["5","R5","E ∧ K","Yes","H, K, A, F, E, B"],["6","R2","B","Yes","H, K, A, F, E, B, D"],["7","R6","D ∧ E ∧ K","Yes","H, K, A, F, E, B, D, C: goal reached"]],"legende":"Rules fired: R3, R1, R5, R2, R6. Final base: {H, K, A, F, E, B, D, C}.","notes":"Goal C is established during the first pass over the rules. R4 and R7 never fire, since G is neither in the initial base nor deducible. Variant: if the engine goes back to the first rule after each firing, as in the role play, you get the same firings and the same final base; only the number of rules examined increases."},{"type":"question","titre":"Demonstration: what if G were known?","question":"We add G to the initial base: {H, K, G}. What happens?","reponse":"Goal C is reached earlier, through **R4**: R3, then R1, then R4, since E and G are known.","explication":"R7 becomes applicable, but adds nothing new: A is already in the base.","notes":"10 min. Open the interactive demonstration in the lesson “Symbolic AI: expert systems”: H and K are ticked; move forward with “Examine the next rule” and have students comment on each step. Then have them predict the effect of G before ticking its box. The demonstration then marks R7 as “useless”; the tutorial’s Python program, on the other hand, counts it as fired (R3, R7, R1, R4). In both cases, C comes through R4."},{"type":"points","titre":"Common mistakes","points":["Firing a rule when only some of its premises are known","Forgetting one of the conclusions of R3: A **and** F","Firing a rule a second time, or ignoring the required order","Trying to deduce G: it is a non-deducible fact"],"notes":"Go back over these mistakes using the grids observed during question b. For each one, ask the class which instruction of the engine was forgotten."},{"type":"question","titre":"Wrap-up","question":"In one sentence: what is the difference between forward and backward chaining?","reponse":"Forward chaining starts from the known facts and applies the rules until it reaches the goal; backward chaining starts from the goal and works back to the facts needed to establish it.","notes":"5 min. Everyone writes their sentence on a piece of paper before the answer is revealed; collect them as exit tickets: they will tell you whether the concept has been grasped."},{"type":"fin","titre":"Homework","points":["Redo exercise 1 without the solution, three days from now","Write five rules that distinguish the fable, the tale and the short story","Work them through with forward chaining on two well-known texts"],"texte":"Next session: machine learning.","notes":"For the five rules, remind students of the session’s model: observable premises (length, presence of a moral, characters, supernatural elements…) and a conclusion. Faced with real texts, students will see how fragile rules are outside the cases they were designed for: this is the limit of expert systems highlighted in the lesson."}]},{"id":"seance-7","numero":7,"titre":"Machine learning: k-means, regression, gradient descent","resume":"Define the vocabulary of machine learning, run k-means by hand until it stops, and calculate two steps of gradient descent while explaining the role of the learning rate.","duree":"1 h 30 min","lien":"cours.html#c3-algorithmes","fiche":"enseignant.html#guide-seance-7","diapos":[{"type":"titre","surtitre":"Chapter 3 · Session 7","titre":"Machine learning","sousTitre":"k-means, linear regression, gradient descent","notes":"A 1 h 30 min session in six phases: from rules to examples (10 min), k-means by hand (20), demonstration (10), regression (15), gradient descent (25), wrap-up (10). Prerequisites: sessions 3 and 6, being able to calculate a mean. Before class, check that everyone has a calculator and that the demonstrations in the lesson “Some machine learning algorithms” open on the classroom computer."},{"type":"points","titre":"Session objectives","points":["Define **features**, **label**, **model**, **training** and **test**","Run **k-means** by hand until it stops","Calculate two steps of **gradient descent**","Explain the role of the **learning rate** η"],"notes":"Read the objectives aloud: they are action verbs, and they can be checked before the end of the session. Announce the method: each algorithm is first done by hand, on very small numbers, then with the site’s demonstration. “If you can redo the calculation by hand, you have understood the algorithm.”"},{"type":"question","titre":"From rules to examples · 10 min","question":"Could you write down the rules that make you recognize Kateb Yacine’s prose?","reponse":"Hardly: you recognize it within a few lines, but you cannot list the rules behind that judgment.","explication":"The expert system of session 6 required rules written by hand. Machine learning reverses the approach: you give the program examples, and it finds regularities in them by itself.","notes":"Allow two or three minutes. Write the “rules” suggested by the students on the board and have them notice that these rules remain vague or incomplete. Then reveal the answer. Transition: much knowledge resists being turned into rules; this is the starting point of machine learning."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Machine learning (ML)","traduction":"apprentissage automatique","definition":"You give the program examples, and it finds regularities in them by itself. Learning means adjusting the **parameters** of a model, that is, numbers, until it reproduces the known examples as well as possible.","exemple":"Tom Mitchell (1997), paraphrased: a program learns when its performance on a task improves with experience.","notes":"Stress the word “parameters”: they are numbers, not rules. The whole session shows ways of finding numbers: group centers for k-means, a slope and an intercept for regression, successive steps for gradient descent."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Vocabulary, using the example of a spam filter","entetes":["Term","In French","In a spam filter"],"lignes":[["Features","attributs","number of words, presence of the word “free”"],["Label","étiquette","“spam” or “non-spam” (supervised only)"],["Model","modèle","the formula that turns the features into a prediction"],["Training","entraînement","adjusting the parameters on examples"],["Test set","jeu de test","emails never seen before, to evaluate the predictions"]],"legende":"The corpus is split: often 70 to 80% of the examples for training, the rest for the test.","notes":"Have the students find the example for each row before you show it. Key point: a model is never evaluated on the examples used to train it, just as a student is not judged on questions whose answers they know by heart. Point out that labels exist only in supervised learning: k-means, which comes next, has none."},{"type":"section","numero":1,"titre":"k-means by hand","sousTitre":"Exercise 2 d · 20 min","notes":"Recall the principle: forming groups of similar objects without any labels. This is unsupervised learning, and the tool for mini-project subject 2 on surveys."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Exercise 2 d: six characters, two groups","duree":15,"consignes":["Six characters in a play: 1, 3, 4, 9, 10 and 14 lines (fictitious data).","Place them on a number line; k = 2 groups, starting centers μ₁ = 1 and μ₂ = 4.","**Assign**: each point joins the nearest center.","**Recompute**: each center becomes the mean of the points in its group.","Repeat until no point changes group any more."],"materiel":"Calculator; a number line from 0 to 15 on the board","notes":"Draw the line and have a student place the six points. Walk around during the calculation: the most common mistake is stopping after one iteration, or forgetting to recompute the centers. Keep 5 minutes for the correction."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Solution: three iterations","entetes":["Iteration","Centers","Group 1","Group 2","New centers"],"lignes":[["1","μ₁ = 1; μ₂ = 4","{1}","{3, 4, 9, 10, 14}","1 and 40 / 5 = 8"],["2","μ₁ = 1; μ₂ = 8","{1, 3, 4}","{9, 10, 14}","8/3 ≈ 2.67 and 33 / 3 = 11"],["3","μ₁ ≈ 2.67; μ₂ = 11","{1, 3, 4}","{9, 10, 14}","groups unchanged: stop"]],"legende":"Result: {1, 3, 4}, the minor roles, and {9, 10, 14}, the main roles.","notes":"Correct row by row, having the students dictate the distances. Iteration 2: 3 goes to group 1 (distances 2 and 5), and so does 4 (3 and 4); 9, 10 and 14 stay in group 2 (distances to 8: 1, 2 and 6). Iteration 3: distances to 8/3 of about 1.67, 0.33 and 1.33, distances to 11 of 2, 1 and 3: nothing moves, and the algorithm stops. Point out that one iteration was not enough: after the first one, group 2 still mixed very different roles."},{"type":"question","titre":"Demonstration · 10 min","question":"Run the demonstration again from other starting centers: do you always get the same groups?","reponse":"Not always: the result depends on the starting point, as well as on the choice of k.","explication":"The algorithm measures distances and calculates means without understanding anything about the data. This is why scikit-learn runs it again from several starting points (`n_init`) and keeps the best solution. Naming the groups remains the researcher’s job.","notes":"Open the k-means demonstration in the lesson “Some machine learning algorithms”. Before each click on “Next step”, have the students predict the assignments; then draw new centers and compare. Possible long version (40 min): the novel cards activity in the guide, where starting from E, H and I leads to a poor solution."},{"type":"section","numero":2,"titre":"Linear regression","sousTitre":"Exercise 2 a · 15 min","notes":"A change of family: we go back to supervised learning. The aim is no longer to form groups, but to predict a number from examples whose answer is known."},{"type":"activite","titre":"A line drawn by eye","duree":5,"consignes":["Five students (fictitious data): x = hours of reading per week, y = books read in the semester.","Points: (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 5), (4, 4), (5, 6).","Plot them on a pair of axes and draw by eye the line that comes closest to them.","Compare with your neighbor: which line is the best, and by what criterion?"],"materiel":"A sheet of paper, a ruler","notes":"Let them draw without a formula. The lines differ from one student to another: this is the moment to ask for an objective criterion. Expected: the line whose differences from the points are the smallest. This leads into least squares."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Linear regression","traduction":"régression linéaire","definition":"Predicting a number with the line ŷ = w · x + b. The **least squares** method chooses w and b so that the mean of the squared differences (yᵢ − ŷᵢ)² is as small as possible.","exemple":"In exercise 2 a, the slope is written a: ŷ = a · x + b.","notes":"Read ŷ as “y hat”: it is the predicted value. w is the slope, b the starting value. Squaring stops positive and negative differences from canceling each other out, and penalizes large errors. There is no need to do the whole calculation in class: give the result (next slide); the full calculation is part of the homework."},{"type":"chiffre","valeur":"6.7 books","legende":"predicted for 6 hours of reading per week: ŷ = 0.9 × 6 + 1.3, with a = 0.9 and b = 1.3","source":"Exercise 2 a · fictitious data","notes":"The calculation, if you are asked: x̄ = 3 and ȳ = 4; Σ(x − x̄)(y − ȳ) = 9 and Σ(x − x̄)² = 10, so a = 9 / 10 = 0.9 and b = 4 − 0.9 × 3 = 1.3. Reading: each extra weekly hour is associated with 0.9 more books over the semester, in these fictitious data. Warning: x = 6 lies outside the observed range (1 to 5); it is an extrapolation. Common mistake: extending the line far beyond the observed data."},{"type":"section","numero":3,"titre":"Gradient descent","sousTitre":"Exercise 2 b · 25 min","notes":"Opening question: most models cannot be solved in one go; how do we find their parameters? Answer: step by step, with gradient descent."},{"type":"deux","titre":"The hiker in the fog","colonnes":[{"titre":"The hiker","points":["Cannot see the valley","Feels the slope under their feet","Takes one step downhill each time","Adjusts the length of their step"]},{"titre":"The algorithm","points":["The valley: the cost function J(w), which measures the error","The slope: the gradient, the derivative of J","The step: w ← w − η · slope","η (eta): the **learning rate** (*taux d’apprentissage*)"]}],"progressif":true,"notes":"Tell the metaphor, then reveal the lines one by one. The arrow means “replace with”. The tricky point is the sign: if the slope is positive, w decreases; if it is negative, w increases. Either way, you go downhill. Possible long version: the activity with eleven sheets laid on the floor (guide, 25 min)."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Exercise 2 b in pairs","duree":10,"consignes":["J(w) = (w − 3)², smallest at w = 3; its derivative is J′(w) = 2(w − 3).","Start at w₀ = 0; learning rate η = 0.1.","Calculate w₁ and w₂ with wₖ₊₁ = wₖ − η · J′(wₖ).","Calculate J(w₀), J(w₁) and J(w₂). Toward which value does the sequence move?"],"materiel":"Calculator","notes":"The derivative is given: we assess the algorithm, not differential calculus. Walk around and watch the sign: 0 − 0.1 × (−6) does give +0.6. Pairs who finish early calculate w₃."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Solution to exercise 2 b","entetes":["Step k","wₖ","J(wₖ)","Slope J′(wₖ)","wₖ₊₁ = wₖ − 0.1 × J′(wₖ)"],"lignes":[["0","0","9","2 × (0 − 3) = −6","0 + 0.6 = 0.6"],["1","0.6","5.76","2 × (0.6 − 3) = −4.8","0.6 + 0.48 = 1.08"],["2","1.08","3.6864 ≈ 3.69","2 × (1.08 − 3) = −3.84","1.08 + 0.384 = 1.464"]],"legende":"The error decreases at each step. The distance to the minimum is multiplied by 0.8 (3, 2.4, 1.92…): the sequence tends toward w = 3.","notes":"Have the students read the J column: 9, then 5.76, then about 3.69; the error decreases. The factor 0.8 comes from rewriting the rule: wₖ₊₁ − 3 = (1 − 2η)(wₖ − 3) = 0.8 (wₖ − 3). The last row goes one step further than the exercise: w₃ = 1.464, as in the solution."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Demonstration: changing the step η","entetes":["η","Values of w from 0","What happens"],"lignes":[["0.1","0.6, 1.08, 1.464…","w gets closer to 3: the algorithm converges"],["1.1","6.6, −1.32, 8.18…","w jumps over the minimum and moves away from it: it diverges"],["0.01","0.29 after five steps","w barely moves: it is too slow"]],"legende":"A larger step does not always go faster.","notes":"Open the gradient descent demonstration (lesson “Some machine learning algorithms”) and have the students predict before each try: η = 0.1, then 1.1, then 0.01. Have them state the moral: when it is too large, the step jumps over the minimum; when it is too small, learning is very slow. The solution to exercise 2 b adds a borderline case: with η = 1, w oscillates endlessly between 0 and 6."},{"type":"question","titre":"Checking the sign","question":"If the slope is negative, what happens to w at the next step?","choix":["It increases","It decreases","It does not change"],"reponse":"It increases","explication":"w ← w − η · slope: subtracting a negative number amounts to adding. In exercise 2 b, the slope is −6 and w goes from 0 to 0.6.","notes":"Take a vote by show of hands before revealing the answer. This is the exit ticket question that the guide suggests for this notion; if many students get it wrong, go back over the first row of the solution table."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Wrap-up · 10 min: three algorithms","entetes":["Algorithm","Supervised or not?","What it produces"],"lignes":[["k-means","Unsupervised","groups, which the researcher interprets and names"],["Linear regression","Supervised","a number predicted by the line ŷ = w · x + b"],["Gradient descent","Neither: it is a method","the parameter values that reduce the error"]],"notes":"Build the table with the class before displaying it: one student per row comes to the board. Expected for gradient descent: it is not a model, but a way of finding the parameters of a model, step by step. It is also used to correct the weights of neural networks, which are on the agenda for the next session."},{"type":"points","titre":"Common mistakes","points":["Stopping k-means after one iteration, or forgetting to recompute the centers","Extending a regression line far beyond the observed data","Getting the sign wrong in w ← w − η · slope","Believing that a larger step always goes faster"],"notes":"Go over them again with the class: each one came up during the session (iteration 1 of exercise 2 d, x = 6 in exercise 2 a, the slope −6 and η = 1.1 in exercise 2 b)."},{"type":"fin","titre":"Homework","points":["Finish exercises 2 a, 2 b and 2 d, then do exercise 2 c (k-nearest neighbors)","Mini-project subject 2: read `kmeans_enquete.py` and the elbow method"],"texte":"Next session: Transformers and generative AI. Before then, read through the lesson “Deep learning”.","notes":"Remind the students of the tutorial (TD) instruction: work on each exercise for at least twenty minutes before opening the solution. For subject 2: the elbow method keeps the value of k after which the inertia hardly decreases any more (k = 3 in the lesson’s example)."}]},{"id":"seance-8","numero":8,"titre":"Transformers and generative AI","resume":"Explain token, embedding and attention, describe text generation as the repeated prediction of the next token, and check a reference produced by a chatbot.","duree":"1 h 30 min","lien":"cours.html#c3-deep","fiche":"enseignant.html#guide-seance-8","diapos":[{"type":"titre","surtitre":"Chapter 3 · Session 8","titre":"Transformers and generative AI","sousTitre":"How a machine writes, and why it must be checked","notes":"Outline: guessing the next word (15 min), tokens, embeddings, attention (20), demonstration (20), the reference test (15), ethics (15), wrap-up (5). The day before: install `transformers` and `torch` and download the models (or prepare Colab), take screenshots of the outputs as a backup, and ask a chatbot for five references on a specific topic. Prerequisites: session 7 and the lesson “Deep learning” read through."},{"type":"points","titre":"Session objectives","points":["Explain **token**, **embedding** and **attention**","Describe generation as the **repeated prediction of the next token**","**Check** a reference produced by a chatbot"],"notes":"Announce the thread of the session: first the class plays the role of a language model, then we open the box, run real models, and finally put them to the test."},{"type":"section","numero":1,"titre":"Guessing the next word","sousTitre":"15 min, without a computer","notes":"No theory for now: the students will do themselves what a generative model does."},{"type":"question","titre":"Complete it out loud","question":"“Il pleure dans mon…”","reponse":"“cœur”: Verlaine, *Romances sans paroles* (1874).","explication":"Everyone gives the same word: in this context, only one continuation is very likely.","notes":"Ask for a collective answer. Expected: “cœur”, unanimously. This is the poem whose words were counted in Python in the chapter 2 tutorial (TD) (exercise 3). Question to ask: how do you know? Because you have already read this sequence. In the same way, a language model estimates the probability of a sequence of words, based on a very large number of texts."},{"type":"activite","titre":"“The student opened the book and…”","duree":7,"consignes":["Everyone secretly writes down the next word, then reads it out loud.","On the board: each suggestion and the number of students who gave it.","This table is a **probability distribution** over the next word."],"materiel":"Half a sheet of paper per student; the board","notes":"Write down each suggestion with its count. This time, the answers differ: several continuations are plausible. Have the students say that the most frequent one is not the only possible one. Leave the table visible for the next slide."},{"type":"deux","titre":"Choosing the next word: temperature","colonnes":[{"titre":"Low temperature","points":["Always take the most frequent suggestion","Predictable text","T < 1: the differences between probabilities grow"]},{"titre":"High temperature","points":["Draw at random among the suggestions","Surprising, then incoherent text","T > 1: the differences flatten out"]}],"notes":"Do both draws with the class’s table: once by taking the most frequent word, once at random (slips of paper drawn from a box, for example). A model does exactly this, token after token. The lesson’s formula, p(tokenᵢ) = exp(zᵢ / T) / Σⱼ exp(zⱼ / T), is not required."},{"type":"section","numero":2,"titre":"Tokens, embeddings, attention","sousTitre":"20 min","notes":"Follow the lesson “Deep learning”, part “Text generation with Transformers”. Some context: in 2017, the paper “Attention Is All You Need” (Ashish Vaswani and his co-authors, Google) introduced the Transformer, which processes all the words of a passage at the same time and computes the relations between them with an attention mechanism."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Token","traduction":"token (jeton)","definition":"The unit of text that the model processes: a word, part of a word or a punctuation mark. A rare word is cut into several pieces.","exemple":"The GPT-2 vocabulary has a little over 50,000 tokens.","notes":"Insist on this: the model sees neither letters nor ideas, but a sequence of tokens. It always predicts the next token, not a whole sentence or a ready-made answer."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Embedding","traduction":"plongement","definition":"Each token becomes a vector of several hundred numbers. Words used in similar contexts get vectors that are close to each other.","exemple":"The distributional hypothesis, summed up by J. R. Firth in 1957: “*You shall know a word by the company it keeps*”.","notes":"Explain the quotation in plain words: we recognize a word by the company it keeps. Link this to literary reading: the meaning of a word is also built in its contexts. The vector understands nothing; it summarizes neighborhoods."},{"type":"question","titre":"Attention","question":"“The student handed her thesis to the professor because she had finished.” Which word should the token “she” look at most?","choix":["“thesis”","“professor”","“student”"],"reponse":"“student”","explication":"**Attention**: each token computes how much it should “look at” each of the others, whatever the distance between them. It is the heart of the Transformer.","notes":"Have the students vote, then ask how they know: the meaning of the sentence (in French, the feminine agreement as well). The model learns these links from examples. The formula Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(Q · Kᵀ / √d) · V is in the lesson: no need to go into detail. This mechanism also makes it possible to process all the words in parallel, whereas a recurrent network (RNN) read one word after another."},{"type":"deux","titre":"Two families of Transformers","colonnes":[{"titre":"Encoders: BERT (Google, 2018)","points":["Read the sentence in both directions","Used to **understand**: classification, sentiment, named entities","For French: CamemBERT; for Arabic: AraBERT"]},{"titre":"Decoders: GPT (OpenAI, since 2018)","points":["Read from left to right","Predict the next token","Used to **generate** text"]}],"notes":"Common mistake: confusing BERT, which understands and classifies, with GPT, which generates. Link this to the mini-project: sentiment analysis (subject 1) and the named entities of a political speech (subject 3) are tasks for encoders."},{"type":"etapes","titre":"Generating means looping","etapes":[{"titre":"Compute","texte":"The model gives a probability to each token in the vocabulary."},{"titre":"Draw","texte":"A token is drawn at random according to these probabilities, adjusted by the temperature."},{"titre":"Append","texte":"The drawn token is added to the end of the text."},{"titre":"Repeat","texte":"The lengthened text becomes the new starting point."}],"progressif":true,"notes":"This is the central objective of the session. Have a student rephrase it using the opening game: the class went through these four steps with “The student opened the book and…”. The consequence to draw out: the model does not look for information; it continues a text in a plausible way."},{"type":"section","numero":3,"titre":"Use, check, declare","sousTitre":"Demonstration (20 min) · references (15 min) · ethics (15 min)","notes":"Now we run real models, then put them to the test. If the connection or the installation fails, show the screenshots prepared the day before."},{"type":"code","titre":"Demonstration: generating with GPT-2","code":"from transformers import pipeline, set_seed\n\n# GPT-2 (2019): small generative model, English texts\ngenerateur = pipeline(\"text-generation\", model=\"gpt2\")\nset_seed(42)  # reproducible sampling on the same installation\n\ndebut = \"The old librarian opened the forbidden book and\"\nsorties = generateur(debut, max_new_tokens=40, do_sample=True,\n                     temperature=0.3, num_return_sequences=2)\nfor s in sorties:\n    print(s[\"generated_text\"], end=\"\\n\\n\")","legende":"`generation_texte.py` (lesson), with `temperature=0.3`, then `1.5`. The continuation depends on the seed, the temperature and the installed versions: there is no fixed output.","notes":"Have the students predict before running it: at 0.3, a fairly predictable text; at 1.5, a surprising, then incoherent text. Read the outputs out loud and have the students pick out sentences that are plausible but absurd or false. GPT-2 is an English model released in 2019; for French or Arabic, you need to choose another model on the Hugging Face Hub. About 10 minutes."},{"type":"code","titre":"Demonstration: an English model that classifies","code":"from transformers import pipeline\n\n# English model fine-tuned on film reviews (SST-2)\nmodele = (\"distilbert/distilbert-base-uncased-\"\n          \"finetuned-sst-2-english\")\nanalyseur = pipeline(\"sentiment-analysis\", model=modele)\navis = [\n    \"I loved this novel, the characters are unforgettable.\",\n    \"The ending was a real disappointment.\",\n]\nfor texte, resultat in zip(avis, analyseur(avis)):\n    print(resultat[\"label\"], \"|\", texte)","sortie":"POSITIVE | I loved this novel, the characters are unforgettable.\nNEGATIVE | The ending was a real disappointment.","legende":"`sentiment_transformers.py` (lesson). Then add a sentence in French to the `avis` list: what does this model, trained on English, answer?","notes":"First run it as it is: the two English reviews are correctly classified. Add a French sentence of your choice and run it again in front of the class without announcing the result. Whatever the result, ask the question: can a model fine-tuned on English film reviews judge a French text? Link this to subjects 1 (sentiment) and 3 (political speeches) of the mini-project. About 10 minutes."},{"type":"activite","titre":"The reference test","duree":15,"consignes":["Here are five references suggested by a chatbot on a specific topic.","Each group takes one and looks for it in a library catalog.","Does it exist? Are the author, title, publisher and year correct?","Each group announces its verdict in one sentence."],"materiel":"The five references, requested the day before, printed or projected; access to a library catalog","notes":"Choose a specific topic from your discipline. You can check the references before class, but do not announce the result. If some of them do not exist, show that the tool invented them with confidence; if everything exists, the lesson is about checking itself. A common mistake to name: trusting a reference because it is well presented."},{"type":"definition","terme":"Hallucination","traduction":"hallucination","definition":"A generative model produces the most plausible continuation, not the most truthful one: it can confidently invent a quotation, a date or a complete reference that does not exist.","exemple":"Rule for your thesis: never cite a source that you have not found and read yourself.","notes":"Draw the lesson from the test: a reference that does not exist, in a thesis, is a scholarly fault, whatever its origin. Every quotation is checked in the original edition."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Ethics: three rules for the class","duree":15,"consignes":["Declaring the use of AI in a thesis: what must be stated?","Uploading texts online: which ones must never be uploaded?","Darja, Tamazight: what can be done when a language is handled less well?","Write three rules together, one sentence each."],"materiel":"The lessons “Outlook: opportunities, limits and ethics” and “Learning with an AI assistant, without cheating”","notes":"Expected answers, based on the lessons. Declaring: the name of the tool, its version, the date, the precise use and the prompts given; presenting a generated text as your own is plagiarism. Never upload personal data, or unpublished or protected texts, to a service that reuses content. Languages that are poorly represented on the Web, such as Darja or Tamazight, are handled less well than English: check the results on a sample. Write the three rules on the board."},{"type":"points","titre":"Common mistakes","points":["Believing that the model knows or searches: it continues a text in a plausible way","Trusting a reference because it is well presented","Confusing BERT, which understands and classifies, with GPT, which generates"],"notes":"Have the students link each mistake to a moment of the session: the next-word game, the reference test, the two families of Transformers."},{"type":"fin","titre":"Wrap-up · 5 min","texte":"Complete on half a sheet of paper: “For a model, generating text means…”","points":[{"texte":"**Homework**","sous":["Reread the “Hallucinations and sources” box, then write your own rule for using an AI assistant in five lines","For the group concerned: prepare the round table “AI as a source of new issues”"]}],"notes":"Collect the completed sentences. Expected: predicting, token after token, a plausible continuation; computing probabilities, drawing a token, adding it, starting again. Open the next session with the most frequent answers."}]},{"id":"mini-projet","titre":"Launching the mini-project","resume":"Choose a mini-project subject as a group, knowing the approach, the possible data, the deliverables, the schedule, the oral presentation and the assessment.","duree":"3 weeks","lien":"cours.html#mini-projet","diapos":[{"type":"titre","surtitre":"Mini-project · supervised independent work","titre":"The mini-project","sousTitre":"Five subjects, six groups, three weeks for a small but complete piece of research","notes":"To be shown at the end of chapter 2: this is when the syllabus plans to hand out the mini-project, for three weeks of supervised independent work. Before the session, set the rule on the number of subjects, the due date, how the work is submitted and the length of the oral presentation, and check that a data source is accessible for each subject. Everything labeled “suggested” comes from the guide and should be adapted."},{"type":"points","titre":"A small but complete piece of research","points":["Ask a research question","Gather data","Process them in Python","Interpret the results with the critical eye of your discipline","Three outputs: a **notebook**, a **report**, an **oral presentation**"],"progressif":true,"notes":"Present the mini-project as research in miniature, not as a programming exercise. The last step, interpretation, is where literature students have an advantage: the tool produces clues, and it is the research question that gives them meaning."},{"type":"deux","titre":"One subject or three? The syllabus says both","colonnes":[{"titre":"Presentation of the mini-project","points":["“Work on 1 subject of your choice from” the five subjects"]},{"titre":"Teaching method","points":["“Mini-project (complete at least 3 of the 5 proposed subjects)”"]}],"notes":"The official syllabus gives two different instructions: do not leave the students in doubt. Announce here the rule you have chosen, with your head of department if necessary, write it on the board and keep it all semester. If you choose three subjects, the guide suggests spreading them from week 4 to week 9, one subject every two weeks. If the rule is one subject, you can allow the fastest groups to work on a second one."},{"type":"section","numero":1,"titre":"The five subjects","sousTitre":"Methods, tools, data","notes":"Each subject has its own page on the site: objective, possible research question, data, deliverables, tools listed in the syllabus, step-by-step approach and starter code."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Five subjects to choose from","entetes":["Subject","Method","Tools listed in the syllabus"],"lignes":[["1. Sentiment analysis on social media","Polarity of texts: positive, negative or neutral","TextBlob, VADER, pandas, matplotlib"],["2. Survey clustering","k-means: groups formed without labels","pandas, scikit-learn, seaborn"],["3. Political speeches","Named entities and topics (LDA)","spaCy, BERT, gensim, WordCloud"],["4. Predictive modeling","Logistic regression: estimating an outcome (yes or no)","scikit-learn, pandas, seaborn"],["5. Network analysis","Graph of relationships, centrality measures","NetworkX, Gephi, pandas"]],"legende":"Details of each subject on the site’s “Mini-project” page.","notes":"Reassure the students: the methods will be covered in chapter 3, during the mini-project, and each subject page provides starter code. The syllabus lists both sklearn and scikit-learn: they are the same library. For subject 1, Tweepy is hardly usable (the X API has been paid since 2023), and GeoPandas is only useful if the texts are linked to a place."},{"type":"question","titre":"One question: which subject?","question":"“Can we see the fragmented structure of *Nedjma* in the network of its characters?” Which subject does this research question belong to?","choix":["Subject 1 · Sentiment","Subject 2 · Clustering","Subject 3 · Political speeches","Subject 4 · Prediction","Subject 5 · Networks"],"reponse":"Subject 5 · Networks","explication":"The characters are the nodes and their shared appearances the links; centrality measures show who connects the groups and who stays on the margins.","notes":"Then read the other questions suggested on the site. Subject 1: how did internet users receive the film or TV adaptation of a novel? Subject 2: are there distinct reader profiles among Master’s students? Subject 3: which themes and which places dominate the speeches of the UN general debate? Subject 4: which factors are associated with students’ attendance at cultural events? Each group can draw on them, but must formulate its own question."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Where to find the data","entetes":["Subject","Possible data"],"lignes":[["1. Sentiment","Sentiment140, Allociné, LABR; public comments collected by hand"],["2. Clustering","Your anonymous questionnaire; World Values Survey, Arab Barometer"],["3. Speeches","Official websites, vie-publique.fr, UN General Debate Corpus"],["4. Prediction","Your questionnaire (a yes/no question); UCI public datasets"],["5. Networks","A link table noted while reading; NetworkX sample networks"]],"legende":"For each dataset: who produced it, where, when, and how it was collected.","notes":"A questionnaire (subjects 2 and 4) requires the respondents’ consent and anonymity, and it takes time for people to fill it in: it must go out in the first week. With only a few dozen answers, conclusions remain very cautious. For subject 5, *Nedjma* is protected by copyright: share the link tables, not the digitized text. If a source seems uncertain to you, plan a small dataset to provide."},{"type":"activite","titre":"Assigning the subjects to the six groups","duree":20,"consignes":["Each group rereads the five subjects on the site and picks three, in order of preference.","Criteria: an interesting question, accessible data, the group’s equipment.","The spokesperson announces the three choices; the teacher arbitrates on the board.","Each group writes down its subject and a first idea for a research question."],"materiel":"The site’s “Mini-project” page (phone or computer room); the grid on the next slide","notes":"A suggested procedure, not a rule of the syllabus: adapt it. Six groups for five subjects: with the one-subject rule, one subject will be taken by two groups, who can choose different corpora and compare their results in the oral presentation. With the three-subject rule, each group picks three and you make sure they are balanced. Equipment: subjects 1 and 3 require heavy libraries (transformers, torch, spaCy) and a good connection; subject 5 requires installing Gephi separately. A group without a computer can work in Colab or in the computer room."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Subject assignment","entetes":["Group","Subject(s)","First idea for a question","Planned data"],"lignes":[["Group 1","","",""],["Group 2","","",""],["Group 3","","",""],["Group 4","","",""],["Group 5","","",""],["Group 6","","",""]],"legende":"A grid to copy onto the board or to print; each row is approved by the teacher during the first week.","notes":"Fill in the grid on the board during the arbitration, then post it in the class space (Moodle or messaging group): it becomes the shared reference. The PDF export of the slideshow also lets you print it blank."},{"type":"points","titre":"Working in a group","points":["Rotating roles: secretary, spokesperson, timekeeper","The less confident student at the keyboard; the other guides, and you swap regularly","A dated **logbook**: what you tried, what failed, what you decided and why","Every member can explain any cell of the notebook","Progress is shown to the teacher every week"],"notes":"These tips come from the guide and from the “Study method” page. For group work, the guide suggests marking the code and the report collectively, and the oral presentation individually: each member answers a question on a part they did not present. If you adopt this principle, announce it today: it prevents a single student from writing all the code."},{"type":"section","numero":2,"titre":"Method and schedule","sousTitre":"Three weeks, six steps","notes":"The approach is the same for all subjects; each subject page then details it step by step."},{"type":"etapes","titre":"Six steps in three weeks","etapes":[{"titre":"W1 · Choose and define the scope","texte":"A precise research question, one or two hypotheses, approved by the teacher."},{"titre":"W1 · Gather the data","texte":"Collect, write a source sheet, anonymize, load into a pandas table."},{"titre":"W2 · Explore and clean","texte":"Count, sort, find duplicates and missing values; write down each decision."},{"titre":"W2 · Apply the method","texte":"The subject’s tool or algorithm, checked on a small sample that you read yourself."},{"titre":"W3 · Interpret and discuss","texte":"Go back to the texts or the data; identify biases and limitations."},{"titre":"W3 · Write and present","texte":"Write the report, rerun the whole notebook, prepare the oral presentation."}],"progressif":true,"notes":"W1, W2 and W3 are the three weeks of the mini-project. Advice from the “Study method” page: start small; a corpus of fifty texts that works is better than ten thousand that do not. The step most often forgotten is checking on a sample read by hand: insist on it."},{"type":"points","titre":"Rules for all subjects","points":["**Sources**: who produced the data, where, when, how it was collected","**Privacy**: remove names, usernames and identifiers; respondents’ consent","**Rights**: terms of use and copyright; analyzing is not republishing","**Reproducibility**: another person can rerun the notebook and get the same results","**Transparency**: any use of an AI assistant is declared, and everything it produces is checked"],"notes":"These five rules appear on the mini-project page and apply to all subjects. Privacy: in Algeria, Law No. 18-07 of 10 June 2018 governs the processing of personal data. Rights: a recent novel may be analyzed, but not republished online. Transparency: the guide suggests ending each report with a declaration of use of AI tools (tool, date, use, prompts, checking), even if the answer is “none”."},{"type":"chronologie","titre":"Suggested schedule (to adapt)","evenements":[{"date":"Week 3","texte":"End of chapter 2: the mini-project is handed out"},{"date":"Week 4","texte":"Define the scope, gather the data; research questions approved"},{"date":"Week 5","texte":"Explore, clean, apply the method; progress check"},{"date":"Week 6","texte":"Interpret, write up; submit the notebook and the report"},{"date":"Week 7","texte":"Individual feedback on the mini-projects"},{"date":"Week 8","texte":"Preparing the oral presentations"},{"date":"Week 10","texte":"Oral presentations"}],"notes":"This is the schedule that the guide suggests over thirteen weeks, for the one-subject rule. Only the handing out at the end of chapter 2 and the three-week duration come from the syllabus. Replace the week numbers with the dates of your faculty’s calendar, and announce the due date, how the work is submitted and the length of the oral presentation. With three subjects, spread them from week 4 to week 9."},{"type":"section","numero":3,"titre":"Deliverables, oral presentation, assessment","sousTitre":"What you hand in, and how it will be assessed","notes":"Hand out the assessment grid today, together with the assignment: that way, the students know what counts."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"What you hand in","entetes":["Deliverable","What is expected"],"lignes":[["Jupyter notebook","Runs without errors from start to finish after a kernel restart"],["Data","Attached to the notebook, or how to obtain them explained precisely"],["Report (4 to 6 pages)","Question, data, method, results, interpretation, limitations"],["Charts","Numbered, given a title and discussed in the text of the report"],["Oral presentation","Your question, your main result, one limitation of your approach"]],"legende":"Due date, length of the oral presentation and how to submit: set by the teacher.","notes":"A Jupyter notebook mixes code cells, results and explanatory text. Suggested outline for the report: research question, data, method, results, interpretation, limitations and ethical issues, references. Each subject adds its own deliverables, specified on its page: a hand-annotated sample (subject 1), a variable dictionary (subject 2), a table of entities (subject 3), a comparison with a naive model (subject 4), a GEXF file (subject 5)."},{"type":"points","titre":"The oral presentation","points":["Present your question, your main result and one limitation of your approach","“Show the cell that cleans the data and explain each line.”","“Change k = 3 to k = 4 in front of me: what changes?”","“Name three messages that your model misclassified and explain each error.”"],"progressif":true,"notes":"The last three lines are examples of questions that the guide suggests for the oral presentation: only the author of a piece of work can answer them, which makes the oral presentation more convincing than a detector of generated text. Warn the students today: it is the best incentive to understand every line of their notebook. The length of the oral presentation is for you to set."},{"type":"tableau","titre":"Assessment grid (suggested points out of 20)","entetes":["Criterion","Points","“Good” level"],"lignes":[["Research question","3","Precise, linked to SHS or to literature, feasible in three weeks"],["Data and ethics","3","Source, date, collection given; anonymization and cleaning described"],["Method and code","4","Runs from start to finish; code commented; parameters given"],["Results and interpretation","4","Charts with titles and comments; return to the texts or the data"],["Report","3","Recommended outline followed, 4 to 6 pages, complete references"],["Oral presentation","3","Keeps to time, explains a code cell and a limitation"]],"legende":"A suggested marking scheme from the guide; official grading follows the regulations in force. Before handing in your work, review it with the site’s self-assessment grid.","notes":"The syllabus only says “Evaluation (identical to the regulations in force)”: do not announce any percentage that your faculty has not set, and scale these points to the share that the regulations give the mini-project. The other levels (insufficient, fair, very good) are in the guide, under “Assessing students”."},{"type":"deux","titre":"Pitfalls to avoid","colonnes":[{"titre":"Avoid","points":["A mere topic: “feelings on Facebook”","Data without a source, or not anonymized","A notebook that does not run from start to finish","Figures and charts without comment"]},{"titre":"Do","points":["A precise question, with a hypothesis to discuss","A source sheet; anonymized data","Rerun the whole notebook before handing it in","Go back to the texts to interpret the figures"]}],"notes":"The left column reflects the “insufficient” level of the grid, the right column the “good” and “very good” levels. Ask each group which of these pitfalls threatens it most, given its subject."},{"type":"fin","titre":"For next week","points":["Read the whole page of your subject","Formulate a research question and one or two hypotheses","Find an accessible data source and start the source sheet","Start the logbook"],"texte":"The scope of each group’s project is approved by the teacher during the first week.","notes":"Set the time for approving the scope (lab session (TP) or appointment) and remind the students of the class’s single channel for questions. If a group is preparing a questionnaire, ask to see it before it is sent out: consent and anonymity must be included."}]}];
